window.harnessChunks["rows-f2db8d171df3af29.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T18:34:28Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ramannanda9/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">react-agent-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ramannanda9/agent-harness/tree/6b399e57daeef6475f2dc37c11a1e8fc097ca7e4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6b399e57daeef6475f2dc37c11a1e8fc097ca7e4\">6b399e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce task-specific technical, research or tool-using outcomes through distinct model-backed ReAct specialists. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable task intent, uncertain tool outputs, investigation and local implementation decisions under dynamically observed results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select next thought, tool invocation, arguments and final answer in each agent&#39;s permitted domain. - Decision owner: A supplied LLM acting through first-party BaseAgent and registered AgentConfig. Tool registry and human allow gates bound the choices but do not select the answer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentConfig, allowlisted ToolRegistry, ReAct loop, working memory, streaming bus, per-run budget and error/resume state. - Closure path: User goal → router or orchestrator chooses task → BaseAgent interprets observation and chooses action/tool → actual tool result returns to its reasoning loop → agent returns concrete task output for the parent run. - Boundary reachability: The shipped AgentRuntime instantiates BaseAgent for registered configurations in both direct and orchestrated entry points; examples exercise a deterministic provider stub without adding outside orchestration code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model chooses substantive next actions conditional on tool output; the framework configures boundaries but does not deterministically decide task substance. - Evidence: Agent configuration, Agent ReAct phases, tool execution, runtime run paths. - Basis: Source-explicit model-driven ReAct, structurally reachable through public runtime. - Confidence: High structurally; no production-model observations at this frozen ref. - Caveats: The example MockLLM only validates wiring and should not be mistaken for model quality or externally authorized tool execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate concrete interference when multiple S1 task runs use one shared registered agent template and would overwrite each other&#39;s local task and working-memory state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two concurrent task drivers receiving identical/incorrect answers after the second overwrites the first task&#39;s mutable BaseAgent instance; nested stateful delegation can reproduce that corruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate an independent mutable runtime state to each ready task, including per-run steering, working memory, checkpoint scope and stateful nested subagent tool, while preserving a shared run-wide budget. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party Orchestrator and BaseAgent cloneforrun machinery; no autonomous agent negotiates the two task runs&#39; shared-resource conflict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Clone-per-task, separate stateful child tools, task-scoped checkpoints, isolated steering queues, common budget and fan-in result collection. - Closure path: DAG produces simultaneous ready tasks referring to the same registered agent → executor clones per task before launch → independent instruction/memory/action execution → distinct valid outputs returned for the corresponding tasks; later planner branches on each separate result instead of overwritten shared state. - Boundary reachability: Orchestrator.runbatch invokes cloneforrun for each Task in the normal shipped parallel code path. A first-party explicit regression test exercises genuine asyncio interleaving and asserts distinct returned answers and original registry preservation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Specific inter-S1 destructive interference is remedied by a shipped deterministic constructor/enforcement path. A planner&#39;s LLM makes assignments, but does not make the isolation decision or handle competing resource claims. The optional LLM memory reconciler concerns later semantic persistence and must not be borrowed as agent-owned live coordination between these task actors. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent tA and tB operational task runs (even if they share one agent template ID); each has a distinct task, working state and outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Actual structurally demonstrated shared-instance overwrite causing both task actors to report the second task&#39;s answer while falsely succeeding. - Attenuating coordination relation: Require a unique clone of BaseAgent and stateful tools for each task within the fan-in batch, isolate checkpoint and steering scopes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each task retains and executes its own instruction, returns a separate output, and downstream dependent tasks/planner use the correct per-task result. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This directly prevents a demonstrated cross-worker mutable-state collision at the shared instance boundary, not merely ordering a DAG or carrying messages. - Evidence: Concurrent collision regression, nested tool and budget controls, cloneforrun, Orchestrator.runbatch. - Basis: Explicit concrete failure class plus first-party executable mitigation, with structural test coverage. - Confidence: High for the narrow interference class; no evidence of general inter-worker negotiation/portfolio resource fairness. - Caveats: C is not independent autonomous S2 decision rights; other cross-agent conflicts (external tools, shared database, contradictory facts) are not universally resolved by per-agent copies.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current whole-run multiagent commitments by choosing and revising which specialist tasks are to proceed, retry, skip or be replaced when observed results fail. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed/low-confidence S1 results, current goal incompleteness, a DAG no longer suitable for the remaining workload and finite run budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose new task assignments, instructions and dependency/remaining-work configuration in a fresh DAG following failures or low-confidence results. - Decision owner: Model-backed run-level planner/replanner LLM in the shipped Orchestrator; fixed onfailure, max-replan count, validator and BudgetGuard only enforce constraints and trigger thresholds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskResult/evaluation, validated DAG, shared budget, run-state/checkpoints, task statuses, streaming feedback and run-level model prompt. - Closure path: Whole-run task/result overview → low-confidence/error and remaining DAG → model chooses revised tasks/agents → validated new DAG replaces current pending commitments and checkpoints → changed S1 agents/tasks actually execute next → synthesized whole-goal answer. - Boundary reachability: AgentRuntime.buildorchestrator directly wires plannerllm and actual registered agents. executeplanstream calls replan on in-boundary task failures and replaces pending tasks with the returned validated plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the planner LLM removes the discretionary reassignment/revision of remaining run-wide commitments; remaining deterministic scheduler and budget checks can enforce and retry/abort but cannot choose the new goal-specific DAG. - Whole-system current view: Run goal, full selected specialist registry, existing completed and failed task results, and remaining unfinished tasks carried in the replan prompt. - Current-control decision scope: Task commitment/agent assignment and revision of remaining present work, not merely initial decomposition; no evidence of organization-wide staffing/portfolio/budget policy beyond this one-run recursion. - Evidence: Runtime orchestrator wiring, Run-plan ownership, Failure/replan control and pending replacement, LLM plan and replan. - Basis: Explicit live feedback/reallocation path with autonomous model judgment. - Confidence: Medium-high structural, restricted to current goal execution. - Caveats: Only low-confidence/failure triggers drive replanning; decision is bounded by max-replan and registered agents, and real external-provider efficiency/correctness is unmeasured. Opt-in PersistentAgent plan-mode lets a human approve/revise an ordinary step plan, but primarily returns prompt priors, not a separate authoritative whole-system parent current-control loop at this assessed recursion; no S3 (P) modifier is claimed. Plan-mode prior injection.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independent complementary access to operational truth beyond agents&#39; normal self-reported outcomes, with audit findings returned to present control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential fabricated tool outcomes or an overconfident producer report that ordinary run traces/quality signals would fail to challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct first-party independently empowered raw-evidence auditor established in this boundary. - Decision owner: Not established; production specialists and orchestrator are ordinary operational actors, while an optional human plan approver reviews future plan proposal rather than actual independent evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trace/event capture, model response confidence, task validation, HITL tool approval and plan-mode prompts; these are not independent complementary audit owners. - Closure path: No first-party independent evidence acquisition → autonomous auditor verdict → corrective S3 intervention loop shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The synthesized answer and conflict list are derived from producer TaskResult rather than independently inspecting task-primary artifacts; no separate read-only reviewer/remediation control is mandated. - Evidence: Synthesis prompt, synthesis after ordinary task results, approval gate, trace and run events. - Basis: Bounded absence after inspecting shipped orchestration, synthesis, audit-like tracing and HITL. - Confidence: Medium-high for the first-party standard paths. - Caveats: Developers can register a separate auditor as an ordinary specialist, but no shipped independent review contract plus corrective return exists automatically.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Externally oriented prospective assessment and option generation to change future system capability, returned to current S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in future user demands, models, tools or environment that would require a prospective capability/organization redesign. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party prospective option right and adoption loop established. Model selection, memory consolidation and replanning change current task execution, not future operational capability. - Decision owner: Not established in the shipped reviewed modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RoutingLLM and ModelSwitcher for configured choices, run-end memory reconciliation, failure replanning, session persistence, background task tools and extensibility hooks. - Closure path: No shipped external/future model → capability-change option → commitment/adoption into present runtime or S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Config-driven model changes and retrospective context writing are not agent-judged external strategic adaptation with installed capability consequences. - Evidence: Routing, manual model switching, Replan prompt only current work, reconciliation for future memory. - Basis: Bounded negative from complete reviewed prospective-looking surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high within the shipped execution cell. - Caveats: The adopter can write custom forecasting/adaptation agents, but the repository does not ship their specific function/authority loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate purpose/identity and ultimate organizational-policy choice balancing current execution and prospective adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ultimate conflict over the goal cell&#39;s persistent mission, acceptable mission changes or constitutional boundary at the organizational recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No source-owned identity-level ultimate-policy decision owner or escalation/return mechanism. - Decision owner: Not established. AgentConfig prompts and allowed tools are adopter inputs; HITL and approval gates cover task/plan/action permission, not authoritative identity revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static role/system prompts, tool policy allow lists, explicit human tool/plan approvals, guardrail values, persistent session configuration. - Closure path: No first-party path from identity-level question to legitimate ultimate authority and returned binding new mission policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A system prompt or safety constraint bounds tasks without constituting a live identity government. Ordinary operator approval is not an identity-level arbitration loop. - Evidence: AgentConfig, tool policy, HITL, plan-mode approval. - Basis: Bounded negative after examining all shipped policy-like/approval surfaces. - Confidence: High for the declared execution-cell boundary. - Caveats: An adopter may establish institutional parent S5 outside this library; absent a first-party identity/ultimate-policy return path it cannot be borrowed here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e86763818d44cb805c4d122b32ce8cfb20ae9def/assessments/re/react-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e86763818d44cb805c4d122b32ce8cfb20ae9def\">e867638 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:06:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aliivihu786/astro-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Astro Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aliivihu786/astro-code/tree/5aae2bddfc364bc492a8cae509b20eb94455560c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5aae2bddfc364bc492a8cae509b20eb94455560c\">5aae2bd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute delegated software investigation and edits against a changing working tree, including when a teammate executes an assigned operational subtask. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requests, tool outcomes, repository state, dependency failures, findings from other teammates and task status changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-selected tool action and next step after actual tool/agent result, independent of the fixed runtime machinery that invokes tools. - Decision owner: first-party Astro Code session model, or a spawned teammate&#39;s model in enabled team mode, acting through Astro-provided execution loops. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt context, query engine, SDK/provider client, tool registry, permissions, task file persistence, communication and run-state UI. - Closure path: user/leader instruction → model/query → selected native tool → execution result → conversation/query continuation and next model decision; teammates also receive assigned tasks and return results/status to their leader. - Boundary reachability: src/entrypoints/cli.tsx initializes the shipped CLI; src/main.tsx and src/QueryEngine.ts bridge actual query results; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx invokes native src/tools/AgentTool/runAgent.ts, rather than merely naming an external model&#39;s independent tool orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static files, locks, provider transports and a CLI argument parser would remain without the model but would not choose context-sensitive coding steps. The model controls operational actions; the human remains the request/approval owner. - Evidence: src/entrypoints/cli.tsx; src/main.tsx; src/query.ts; src/QueryEngine.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/AgentTool/runAgent.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high on source-level operating pathways. - Caveats: static source witnesses are not a benchmark or successful end-to-end execution trace of the large frozen bundle.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: reduce cross-S1 contention over the same outstanding team work, while respecting prerequisite dependencies and incompatible claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two operational teammates attempting to own the same work item, an agent simultaneously claiming overlapping obligations, or a successor task being picked before a blocking predecessor is complete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: atomic admission/rejection of a task claim relative to its owner, status, blockers and agent busy state; subsequent worker selection can attempt another eligible task. - Decision owner: first-party lock/claim protocol selects the conflict response deterministically; teammate/lead models select which task to attempt and can react to refusal, but there is insufficient evidence that an agent autonomously owns the S2-specific conflict regulation judgment. Thus constructor-level C rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proper-lockfile, on-disk task state, dependency edges, owner/status markers, task watcher, background/in-process worker and mailbox notifications. - Closure path: two teammates examine shared work → per-list/per-task lock serializes claim → conflicting/blocked/busy claim rejected without duplicate owner, or valid claim recorded → watcher/runner receives result → worker proceeds only with admitted task or returns to next eligible-work check. - Distinct S1 units: at least two independently executing named team teammates with assigned operational work in the opted-in team mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous claims of the same task and dependency-order interference between their separate work units. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task locks and atomic owner/dependency/busy checks prevent overlapping claims; dependency graph inhibits premature successor work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: src/hooks/useTaskListWatcher.ts and src/utils/swarm/inProcessRunner.ts use claimTask success/failure to determine which task prompt is executed; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts reports newly available/blocked tasks; failed submission releases a claim. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the claim operation explicitly recognizes competing owners and pending predecessor commitments and changes which S1 may act; the relation is not inferred solely from queue or message existence. - Boundary reachability: External users explicitly opt into a first-party team with ASTROCODEEXPERIMENTALAGENTTEAMS or --agent-teams; the supported team creation/AgentTool spawn path supplies actual teammates, and both useTaskListWatcher and inProcessRunner call the shipped claimTask before submitting work. The conflict check is a functional, not hypothetical, local distribution path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models decide their coding work, while fixed claim and lock code supplies collision-specific rejection. No independently evidenced agent right to revise this coordination protocol was established. - Evidence: src/utils/tasks.ts; src/hooks/useTaskListWatcher.ts; src/utils/swarm/inProcessRunner.ts; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: restricted to the explicitly opted-in first-party team mode; manual TaskUpdate direct owner edits are not themselves guaranteed atomic claim operations.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the active team as a whole through owner/commitment allocation and responses to changing team status or blockers, not just assign one task for execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: several active teammates with outstanding or blocked work, mismatched worker/tool capacity, idle workers, dependency bottlenecks and disputed responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model chooses team composition and current work distribution, assigns/reassigns owners, adds dependencies, follows completion/blocked status, sends corrections to peers and authorizes shutdown/handoff of workers. - Decision owner: model-guided team-lead at the local team recursion. Static tools persist decisions but do not select which obligation/worker/priority should change. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamCreate, TaskList, TaskUpdate, SendMessage, team roster, idle/status notifications and execution/worker lifecycle. - Whole-system current view: team roster, task list with ownership, status and blockedBy, team messages and idle/completion signals accessible to the lead rather than only a single worker&#39;s transcript. - Current-control decision scope: resource/worker assignment, reassignment, dependency constraints, requests for help, peer intervention and closing down the current team when work finishes. - Closure path: teammate result/blocked or idle signal → lead&#39;s shared current-team view and model judgment → owner/dependency/message/team roster update → next teammate claim/prompt/coding action reflects the lead&#39;s revised commitment. - Boundary reachability: In the explicitly enabled local first-party team mode TeamCreate registers the leader and shared task list, AgentTool starts named teammates, TeamCreate prompt and TaskList present the whole active obligations, and TaskUpdate/SendMessage return lead allocations and interventions to their teammates. These are not external hosted-controller rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: with the lead model removed the team file and tools can persist old assignments, but they do not autonomously select new priorities or settle which current work to reassign as the global situation changes. - Evidence: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts; src/tools/TeamCreateTool/prompt.ts; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts; src/tools/SendMessageTool/prompt.ts; src/utils/swarm/teamHelpers.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: positive only in first-party opted-in agent-team mode, not in the default single-coder mode. The lead&#39;s coordination authority is over current team obligations, not the user&#39;s organization or product ultimate policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: possible separately instructed agent verification or artifact-review access, with unresolved frozen-distribution availability and consequential return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: generated code or implementation reports could be incorrect, despite routine model narration and tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately running verifier would be able to investigate original claims from raw test/build/artifact evidence and return a challenging PASS/FAIL finding; the frozen code does not establish that this mode is operationally enabled for the public installation. - Decision owner: no confirmed accessible first-party S3 auditor at the declared third-party user boundary. A verification-role model is coded, but gated; the ordinary local /review prompt is executed through the same coder. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/verificationAgent.ts supplies adversarial raw-command checking; src/tools/AgentTool/builtInAgents.ts requires feature(&#39;VERIFICATIONAGENT&#39;) and tenguhiveevidence (false default); src/constants/prompts.ts explicitly describes third-party default as off. src/tools/ReviewArtifactTool/ReviewArtifactTool.ts and src/tools/VerifyPlanExecutionTool/VerifyPlanExecutionTool.ts are throwing compatibility stubs. - Closure path: unresolved for supported public operation; if enabled, the built-in verifier&#39;s result would return to its parent and trigger rework/spot check. No supported, enabled source-backed end-to-end receipt was observed in the frozen installation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named adversarial verifier and local Git review instructions are promising but not sufficient to certify independently available complementary access and returned control at the assessed boundary. - Evidence: src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/verificationAgent.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/builtInAgents.ts; src/constants/prompts.ts; src/commands/review.ts; src/commands/review/ultrareviewEnabled.ts; src/commands/review/reviewRemote.ts; src/tools/ReviewArtifactTool/ReviewArtifactTool.ts; src/tools/VerifyPlanExecutionTool/VerifyPlanExecutionTool.ts. - Basis: explicit + unknown. - Confidence: medium on gated implementation, low on available whole-path audit. - Caveats: ? is a specific frozen code/feature availability gap, not a generic lack of independent reviewer. An external remotely billed ultrareview service is not borrowed as a first-party auditor. Positive audit needs a verified public build gate + independently sourced judgment + parent-control return.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party process creates externally prospective adaptation options and negotiates them back into present system capability at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model choice, user task progress, external web results and external remote scheduling change operational input but do not constitute a future capability/adaptation conversation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/task models select current work; remote-agent scheduling is explicitly set up through user/hosted services rather than a first-party forward-looking strategy owner. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at the packaged local organization boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional remote scheduling skill, web and model integrations, task planning and prompt memory. src/proactive/index.ts is a throwing compatibility stub. - Closure path: ordinary task information and human-configured triggers feed execution; no future external distinctions → adaptation option → current management/capability change cycle is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a roadmap, task plan, background agent, model fallback or remote scheduled job does not show an organization selecting adaptation based on prospective environment. - Evidence: src/skills/bundled/scheduleRemoteAgents.ts; src/proactive/index.ts; src/commands/ultraplan.tsx; src/commands/review.ts; src/tools/TaskCreateTool/TaskCreateTool.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium within the inspected frozen public boundary. - Caveats: external organizational systems controlling remotely scheduled agents may have their own S4, but those owners are not inherited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installed identity-level or ultimate-policy arbitration and binding returned governance loop was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local tool permission, plan approval, agent-team enablement and feature gating restrict operations but do not settle the identity/ethos of the assessed organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human user/operator chooses tasks, approves tools and enables modes; developers/feature service set static gates. These are not established ultimate-policy decisions on S3/S4 tensions. - Decision owner: no independently evidenced first-party S5 actor at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool/agent permission schemas, team-tool gate, GrowthBook controls, project configuration and manual interventions. - Closure path: command or tool is accepted/denied inside a current task; there is no witnessed identity/policy issue → legitimate parent judgment → durable binding return affecting the organization&#39;s future conduct. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human consent and compile/runtime access restrictions are not identity-governance, even where the model owns S1 coding and opt-in S3 current team control. - Evidence: src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; src/services/policyLimits/index.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: a broader team or company may make ultimate-policy choices external to this installation, not a first-party supported parent-governed mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2cba0ce660eeed1dde178621a1932b92d1ce04e4/assessments/astro-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2cba0ce660eeed1dde178621a1932b92d1ce04e4\">2cba0ce &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T01:29:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-shell\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gentle-shell</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-shell/tree/407404372516ac0e27fc63e22ed08344f3f64c90\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 407404372516ac0e27fc63e22ed08344f3f64c90\">4074043 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute scoped coding and repository tasks, choosing inspection, edit, verification and subsequent repair actions based on results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous change requests, unknown files, tool failures, failing tests, incomplete output and parent corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: supported Pi model tool loop and first-party focused worker definitions choose what to read/write/run and how to revise the next step. - Decision owner: parent or focused Pi worker model at the task level, subject to bounded edit and consent rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stock Pi agent host, configured packaged worker definitions, pi --mode rpc execution, first-party task/receipt store and result return. - Closure path: user-scoped goal → model chooses tool/child actions → observes tool/check results → revises or returns evidence → parent accepts, redirects or reports partial work. - Boundary reachability: default installed gentle-pi extends Pi&#39;s supported native model loop and registers actually executable subagents; no evaluator-only or mocked agent is needed for the published execution route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model&#39;s choice among investigation/edit/repair actions is discretionary; tool policy bounds effects but does not mechanically select code actions. - Evidence: packaged Pi extension, actual child process launcher, delegated writer contract. - Basis: first-party installable runtime and model-driven worker decision closure. - Confidence: high for structural action ownership, untested performance. - Caveats: Pi SDK and model provider are normal product dependencies; no live agent run was made in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete interference between independently running S1 agents in a shared clone/worktree, particularly duplicate concurrent privileged remediation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a new remediation agent racing an existing queued or live remediation actor in the same worktree, and finite child process slots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the runner rejects a conflicting same-worktree sdd-remediate launch and defers concurrent work to a bounded queue. The model parent can respond to the refusal, but no autonomous S2 conflict-arbitration judgment is established. - Decision owner: deterministic worktree-scoped admission rule and configured queue cap; parent model has a potential orchestration interface but the decisive collision check is fixed code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRunner.run, task store, worktree-root validation, same-cwd remediation check, queue pump, per-child cancellation/status receipts. - Closure path: second S1 remediation launch asks for same worktree → first-party runner detects active conflicting remediation → rejects instead of starting → parent receives error and must preserve/cancel/wait for existing work → later launch may proceed once no actor holds that worktree lane. - Boundary reachability: native remediation launch flows through gentle-agents.ts and AgentRunner.run with the same-worktree collision guard, not just a protocol schema or maintainer test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prevention of inter-worker resource/correction collision is real, but fixed admission and limits rather than a situated agent decide the conflict; C is bounded to the existing S2 relation, not generic delegation. - Distinct S1 units: independent pi --mode rpc child remediation agents on separately queued requests; each would otherwise be empowered to edit/test within the same target worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits, correction/review artifacts and uncertain ownership when both remediation workers target the same repository state. - Attenuating coordination relation: one privileged remediation lane per worktree plus queue/resource admission; a second worker is refused until ownership is resolved. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refused launch cannot start an interfering actor and returns a specific message to the parent; after cleanup the next child starts under the remaining scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: unlike task-list rendering, this check recognizes and blocks a specific clash in authority over shared worktree changes between separate workers. Other subagent status/ACK paths alone would not qualify. - Evidence: deduplicating admission guard, remediation scope authorization, subagent lifecycle. - Basis: shipped narrow cross-S1 interference attenuator without autonomous arbitration owner. - Confidence: moderate: clear conditional code path, limited to sdd-remediate requests and not all repository writers. - Caveats: maxconcurrency alone would only be generic resource limiting; the worktree-specific duplicate remediation refusal is the material positive witness. No conflict-free deployment was empirically tested.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: hold current accountability over the bounded parent-session development campaign: select child responsibilities, inspect their progress, stop/redirect unfinished work and reconcile outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergent child results, changed user scope, stalled worker, ambiguous verification, time/cost pressure and multiple current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model parent chooses when to delegate, who maps/writes/verifies and what to do with returned evidence. The attended human owner can separately inspect all child tasks of the session, stop an owned active child or steer new scope/next work. - Decision owner: model parent in standard agentic orchestration mode (A); interactive user as legitimate local workflow parent (P) through supported TUI/turn controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegated work-unit contracts, subagentrun/status/result/cancel/sendmessage, task widget/overlay, ownership checks, result events that initiate a parent turn. - Closure path: parent sees task plan and child status → selects/scopes writer, verifier or changed action → registered task executes or stops → child outcome/event returns to parent task context and updates current plan/delivery. - Boundary reachability: shipped assets/orchestrator-delegation.md injects parent-mode guidance and the installed gentle-agents.ts registers real actionable tools; TUI shows active same-session tasks and provides stop/scope navigation to the human. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model judges which work to hand off or repair in relation to other commitments, not only mechanically queues known inputs; human task intervention is a separate explicitly supported mode. - Whole-system current view: for the chosen recursion, subagentlisttasks, status, result and the task overlay cover current own-session workers, queued/running state and retained outcomes; cross-session peers are presence-only and not claimed as a whole distributed enterprise view. - Current-control decision scope: delegation topology, worker/verification assignment, stop, continue and reconciled delivery for the current session&#39;s campaign, as opposed to generic concurrency/timeouts. - Evidence: parent orchestration contract, runtime tools, parent task control and UI. - Basis: scoped current-portfolio agentic control and distinct attended human intervention, not broad cross-repository strategic planning. - Confidence: moderate because the model is prompted to own reconciliation but live compliance is not evaluated. - Caveats: the local-session view excludes other process&#39;s action authority; an ACK from a separate session is not completion or control.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: separately audit S3&#39;s live parent orchestration/portfolio-management assertions and return management correction through an independent evidence channel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: errors in allocation, supervising, prioritizing or reconciling the parent-managed set; no S3-audit closure established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: native review lenses, refuter and verification concern the exact code-change candidate, not independent judgments about the parent session&#39;s management choices; their lifecycle and risk classification additionally belong to the separate Go provider. - Decision owner: no independent S3-audit decision owner in the assessed Pi-extension local-recursion mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional candidate-frozen reviewer completions, delegated verifier and immutable review status/receipts from external provider. - Closure path: not applicable as S3; artifact findings return to delivery/repair, not a separate evaluation of S3&#39;s allocation or intervention claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: genuinely separate review of a code diff is important S1 acceptance evidence, but neither its claim nor independent access is directed at the S3 owner&#39;s management reality. - Evidence: provider review ownership, delegated verification boundary, native authority map. - Basis: bounded negative at local parent-session portfolio recursion. - Confidence: high for inspected reviews, moderate for other unenumerated external companion integrations. - Caveats: excludes external Go provider as an independent organizational function; even including its ordinary diff audit would not by itself constitute S3-management audit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C(P) - Function: assess skill deficiencies seen during work and prepare improvements to reusable future worker capability; in an explicit parent mode accept and write improvements for later projects/tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: drifting task methodologies, outdated or conflicting skill activation instructions, cumbersome skill bodies and recurring retrieval/usage mistakes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-led shipped skill-improver generates bounded audit findings/proposed rewrites (C), but it defaults to audit-only. A human asking to apply changes authorizes actual writing of improved SKILL.md, which the registry reindexes for later use (P). No automatic autonomous future-capability adoption or self-selected learning cadence was established. - Decision owner: assistant model for candidate skill auditing; local human owns the distinct affirmative future-capability application decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: packaged skills/skill-improver/SKILL.md, style gate, allowlisted edits, skill registry scanner/watch and project-vs-global precedence. - Closure path: evidence from existing skill usage/audit → model identifies a reusable skill problem and a revised form → person explicitly requests apply → persisted SKILL.md changes → skill-registry notices/scans revision → later task loads matching updated instruction. - Boundary reachability: the improver and registry are both shipped first-party skills/extensions; no Engram installation is required for filesystem skill reuse. The human apply mode is not the default; no autonomous S4 owner is implied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the proposed content is model-authored but selecting whether durable methodology changes govern future tasks is a human decision, so the positive base is only constructor C, with closed explicit parent mode P. - External distinction: observed deficiencies in actual on-disk skill instructions, task audit outcomes and user-supplied correction requirements—not the current worker merely retrying its patch. - Future / prospective distinction: changing skill activation/format affects later independent tasks by changing their available procedure, not just resolving the current deliverable. - Adaptation option generated: recommended corrections to skill metadata, trigger, structure, rules and decision table; safe apply may rewrite the skill and refresh the index. - Path back into current capability / S3: updated skill file becomes the new source of truth, indexed at .atl/skill-registry.md and routed into subsequent delegated work. - Evidence: first-party skill improver, registry operational skill, actual file-scanning registry extension, companion-memory boundary. - Basis: narrow first-party agent-proposal plus user-approved prospective procedural adaptation; no claim of autonomous ongoing learning. - Confidence: moderate; a supported apply protocol and future registry ingestion, not observed actual post-edit gain. - Caveats: skill-improver is invoked for requested skill improvement, not passively after every run; Engram and external adaptive memory products are excluded. This does not establish unsupervised S4=A.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: set persistent permitted scope for the local coding workflow and ultimate authority over consequential operations and how future subagents may operate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts between repository/user restrictions and tool-generated actions, new sensitivity to destructive commands, background autonomy and attempted widening of approval scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: local human owns project/global durable runtime-guardrails.json allow/confirm/block settings and background-subagents.json; per-session review/remediation authorizations and ordinary delivery remain subject to a human-controlled repository policy. - Decision owner: legitimate self-hosted project owner (P), not the code reviewer, delegated worker or remote OSS maintainer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party runtime config decoder, project-over-global precedence, guarded tool intercepts and interactive consent, session/repository-bound approval identity and fail-closed refusals. - Closure path: human establishes project/global normative guardrails and delegation mode → persisted config is read into the installed session → sensitive commands are allowed/confirmed/blocked accordingly → workers act only under the resulting policy; new consequential authority is escalated for approval. - Boundary reachability: extensions/gentle-ai.ts loads project/global guardrail policy on the production Pi path, and lib/background-subagents-policy.ts is called by gentle-agents.ts to choose child scheduling mode. This is durable local normative control, not individual PR acceptance alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the enduring rule contents and ultimate delivery/authorization identity are supplied by the user; the model acts within—not as the author of—those constraints. - Evidence: guardrail policy resolution, background delegation policy, session standing review permission, delivery separation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whose standing development and safety boundaries determine what the installed local coding assistant is allowed to do across later tasks. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: human owner (P); neither the native reviewer nor child tool has authority to rewrite user-confirmed global/project policy. - Return-to-operation path: project/global files set behavior for subsequent tool interception and background delegation; the session/review consent gate halts unauthorized actions. - Basis: durable local operator-owned policy loop with runtime enforcement. - Confidence: moderate-high for scoped standing safety/workflow policy, not external maintainer governance. - Caveats: GENTLEPIAUTONOMOUSMODE=1 is an explicit operator-selected override; this is still parent-granted, not agent-owned ultimate policy. Some actual safe handling depends on the underlying Pi host and external review provider.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/99795066d57e02db7386e2b0dc2420ec91668165/assessments/ge/gentle-shell.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 99795066d57e02db7386e2b0dc2420ec91668165\">9979506 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:24:50+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AlysisAi/alysis-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alysis Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AlysisAi/alysis-code/tree/ff6682acb4a339dbbf9ce687c46374ce536ad641\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ff6682acb4a339dbbf9ce687c46374ce536ad641\">ff6682a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local coding session accepts user task, calls model and host-approved file/shell/verification tools and iterates on returned observations; model-driven focused child sessions can execute scoped coding tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, evolving code and tests, tool errors, sandbox conditions, provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-specific source edits, inspections and verification work after reading direct tool feedback. - Decision owner: First-party provider-driven Alysis Code agent model and separately model-driven bounded child Session where explicitly delegated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI alysis chat/alysis run, turn.core controller, first-party tool assembly, permission/sandbox enforcement, model turn history. - Closure path: Request → model/tool choice → allowed action → observations and completion/repair evidence → next model step/final. - Boundary reachability: Official alysis-code package CLI binds workspace and creates first-party runtime; code paths and documentation agree. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Coding choices are genuinely model-selected; deterministic sandbox and completion constraints do not replace these decisions. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; src/alysiscode/main.py; src/alysiscode/agent/turn/core.py; src/alysiscode/agent/verification.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent simultaneous independently executing child coding sessions from corrupting a shared worktree or merging incompatible work. - Distinct S1 units: subagentrun/subagentspawn create first-party nested sessions with model instructions and their own tools; Forge swarm workers also work in isolated tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two write-capable children working in the same parent repository can race on the same files or overwrite the other&#39;s candidate; read-only helpers may inadvertently mutate the shared root. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared write-capable child runs are serialized; parallel writers use separate detached worktrees, guarded conflict-aware subagentapply, and dependency/release pinning. Swarm batch scheduler requires disjoint precise claimed path scopes; overlap forces a separate batch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A conflicting task is deferred/serialized, and candidate apply returns fail-closed conflict or a successfully integrated patch; parent receives bounded child outcomes before choosing further work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The system regulates concrete interference among concurrently acting child S1s through worktree isolation and overlap-based batch admission, not just assigning tasks or passing messages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent child edits, overlapping file claims, source-worktree release before checker completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select safe concurrent subset, delay incompatible child writes, enforce isolated candidate release/apply. - Decision owner: Deterministic host SubagentWorkspaceProvider, batch scheduler and host admission constructor rather than an independent coordinating model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Detached Git worktrees, overlap checker, release-pin lock, patch conflict detection, staged task dependencies and child registry. - Closure path: Competing child requests → runtime scope/worktree admission → safe execution or deferral → conflict-aware integration → returned parent result. - Boundary reachability: Documented CLI child modes and Forge swarm are implemented in first-party packages; positive claim bounded to concurrent child/isolation modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent may choose delegation, but inter-child safety policy is enforced by the runtime. - Evidence: docs/subagents.md; src/alysiscode/agent/subagentworkspace.py; src/alysiscode/swarmscheduler.py; src/alysiscode/swarmorchestrator.py - Basis: structural / explicit -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Maintain a whole-current Forge run&#39;s task states, dependencies, acceptance, integration health and continuation under a first-party plan/control cycle. - Whole-system current view: Forge canonical plan and task-status ledger cover all current tasks (runnable, blocked, executing, failed, and awaiting integration), explicit dependencies and changed-path/write scope, plus batch integration evidence. The swarm orchestrator assembles this whole-current picture before admitting subsequent units. - Current-control decision scope: Host Forge/Swarm controller authoritatively chooses which child tasks are runnable together, blocks dependencies or overlapping write scopes, accepts/rejects integration verified batches, updates canonical task states, and optionally recomputes the schedule after validated current-run replanning. This authority crosses multiple S1 task workers rather than operating within one producer&#39;s tool turn. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Individual child failure, unmet prerequisites, incompatible candidate branches, integration verification failures, scope drift and blocked tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine runnable task batches, disallow blocked/dependency-unsatisfied work, reject failed integration, record authoritative task state, and recompute tasks/plan when admissible replanning changes it. - Decision owner: Deterministic Forge/Swarm orchestrator control constructor with optional model replanning constrained by validation and run mode; not an independently chartered model S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Canonical plan/run ledger, TaskCandidate/Schedule, explicit task statuses and dependencies, integration-gate verification, replan bookkeeping and merge guards. - Closure path: Whole-run task-status/dependency view → select runnable work → execute children → inspect integration results → approve/reject merge and update task/run states → recalculate plan/schedule for subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: Shipped alysis forge run, forge exec, forge swarm bind this control plane to plan-driven user tasks; ordinary single chat need not run Forge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current plan-level authority is host-owned; it makes binding decisions across multiple child tasks and is therefore C rather than A. One-shot completion gate alone would not warrant organization-level S3. - Evidence: docs/forge.md; src/alysiscode/swarmscheduler.py; src/alysiscode/swarmorchestrator.py; src/alysiscode/forge.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently inspect a produced coding candidate via separate first-party verifier/code-reviewer model session, with direct access to the frozen candidate worktree and consequential feedback for parent admission. - Claim being audited: A completed write-capable child reports a valid modification satisfying the parent&#39;s task and required verification. - Ordinary reporting path: Producing child returns its own bounded summary/tool events; parent could otherwise trust the child-provided report. - Complementary access path: A separate read-only verifier or code-reviewer subagent is launched with workspacefromrun=&lt;writerrunid&gt;, pointing to the completed unreleased Git worktree itself, rather than solely the writer&#39;s claim. Deterministic independent host acceptance/execution evidence is also collected by the completion certificate. - Independence boundary: Separate subagent model invocation and direct candidate-source view provide a first-party complementary route, not independent OpenSiro external reviewer or verified cross-provider separation. - Who acts on findings: Verifying child returns bounded outcome; parent can resume worker for correction or discard, and calls subagentapply only after accepting the verifier result. Host worktree release locks prevent apply/discard while the reading verifier is running. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Hallucinated completion, wrong patch semantics, scope violations, failing verification of produced candidate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read the candidate in a separately bounded model session and make a task-specific verifier judgment; parent uses findings to withhold admission and request repair. - Decision owner: Configured separate verifier/code-reviewer model session (A), assisted by deterministic host evidence gate (C); no automatic universal audit guarantee. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned source-worktree reader, wait/join and dependency chain, read-only roles, candidate release lock, acceptance certificate, verification evidence. - Closure path: Write child finishes → separate verifier reads exact candidate → model verdict returned to parent → parent applies only if accepted or routes back via subagentresume → candidate admitted/discarded. - Boundary reachability: Documented first-party subagentspawn, subagentwait, workspacefromrun, subagentapply are available in supported top-level chat/one-shot modes; this mode must be specifically requested or chosen by the parent and is not Forge default. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The verifier, unlike the deterministic completion gate or same-model adversarial nudge, is a distinct model actor with raw-source access and own evidence-directed judgment. - Evidence: docs/subagents.md; src/alysiscode/agent/subagentexecution.py; src/alysiscode/agent/subagentworkspace.py; src/alysiscode/agent/verification.py; src/alysiscode/agent/turn/core.py - Basis: structural / explicitly documented - Confidence: medium-high for audit opportunity, medium for realized efficacy - Caveats: Verifier call is optional, user/parent-led and can report incomplete or fail; independent model execution does not imply independent outside organization. An adversarialfinalize self-model nudge alone would only be a weaker constructor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently evidenced prospective external environment intelligence that produces and deploys organization-wide future strategic capability changes at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider/model changes, future task needs and learned recurring issues are possible external signals but not shown as an owned prospective intelligence cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Current Forge replanner can amend immediate plan on integration failure, not conduct future environment sensing with capability options. - Decision owner: User/operator policy and current task model/plan repair; no independent S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Replanning, memory, plugins, skill loading, extension docs and historical feedback bundles. - Closure path: Immediate failure → revised current task schedule, not outside-and-then option → accepted future capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current-run replanning is S3; memory and optional extensibility are supporting surfaces rather than prospective strategic intelligence. - Evidence: src/alysiscode/swarmorchestrator.py; src/alysiscode/replanning.py; docs/architecture.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct agent-owned or organization-legitimate ultimate policy and identity deliberation loop with binding returned changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Approval, sandbox escapes, tool scopes and user trust preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human/operator sets security policy, execution mode and approvals; host enforces them. - Decision owner: Operator and host policy machinery, not an S5 authority that deliberates organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox permission modes, trusted configuration restrictions, approval scopes and execution hooks. - Closure path: Tool action → deterministic allow/deny or human approval; no ultimate-policy/identity issue → legitimate deciding organ → binding policy feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Host-owned safety rails and reviewer veto do not create a sovereign identity/policy organ. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; docs/securitymodel.md; src/alysiscode/approvalscope.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3eda0f415511089d2312c1c3838878f4cccdbf79/assessments/alysis-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3eda0f415511089d2312c1c3838878f4cccdbf79\">3eda0f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T00:51:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Prime Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent/tree/66abc2a604fc42a220292a1ca4cf33ee60cb5733\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 66abc2a604fc42a220292a1ca4cf33ee60cb5733\">66abc2a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. First-party model-driven agents autonomously execute coding/research work through tools and the persistent control environment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Recursive spawning, direct messaging and parent/child communication establish delegation and communication, but the reviewed evidence does not identify a specific peer-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation together with an S2-specific attenuation and feedback loop. The earlier proposal&#39;s C treated generic composability as coordination evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The parent agent can inspect the current child registry/status and make discretionary whole-subtree intervention decisions such as spawning additional work, issuing follow-ups and terminating/deleting child work. Those decisions change subsequent current operation; host budgets/lifecycle controls are supporting enforcement.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Quality gates and refinement evidence are normal validation/optimization mechanisms. No sufficiently independent first-party audit actor with complementary access and a distinct audit judgment feeding corrective current control was established at the pin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Continual Harness performs real durable refinement from operational trajectories, but learning/self-improvement alone is not S4. The reviewed path does not establish an outside-and-then environmental intelligence distinction that generates prospective adaptation options and returns them into present capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Base goals, task objectives and parent-supplied configuration constrain the agent but do not form an operationally closed identity/ultimate-policy loop. Parent ownership of a goal by itself is not P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f17df7784f951d191718bb7504b7770132a18a8a/assessments/prime-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f17df7784f951d191718bb7504b7770132a18a8a\">f17df77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T00:11:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pentoshi007/clai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">clai</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pentoshi007/clai/tree/630d85f9eb7c2b5b3e499c503abf5834610c6939\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 630d85f9eb7c2b5b3e499c503abf5834610c6939\">630d85f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended user-directed coding/security-investigation tasks by selecting and iterating real actions on source and observed evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code structure, failing commands/tests, changing files, HTTP/terminal output, incomplete task facts and model errors/limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses relevant operations/tool arguments, reacts to actual tool results, can continue or stop, and can request bounded independent investigation in orchestration mode. - Decision owner: first-party model-directed main clai agent; separately instantiated read-only child model loops own their own bounded investigations while provider inference is external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool schema/routing, permission/scope gates, terminal and FS tools, context recovery, durable task plan, result capture and provider key retry. - Closure path: user objective and current workspace evidence → model selects tool → first-party execution and guard → output/denial/error returned to model → next action, delegation, or final answer. - Boundary reachability: shipped CLI initializes the first-party runner and dispatches model tool calls, while the optional shipped SubagentManager connects first-party model-backed investigator workers; neither depends on a separate external agent binary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model-directed tool/next-action choices leaves deterministic execution and policy checks, not a task-sensitive coding actor. - Evidence: src/agent/runner.ts; src/agent/turn/loop/run-rounds.ts; src/agent/turn/tool-execution/single-tool.ts; src/agent/subagents/worker.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no materially closed shipped coordination of a specific interacting-S1 interference despite parallel context-gathering child loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential duplicate evidence gathering, resource consumption, conflicting conclusions and overlapping investigative effort among children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: SubagentManager.assertUnique blocks an exact repeated assignment before it launches; assertCapacity limits concurrent instances, while prompt guidance asks for unrelated scopes. No worker-to-worker discrepancy/oscillation detection and corrective inter-S1 feedback right is established. - Decision owner: fixed admission guards and task-allocation instructions, not an autonomous S2 conflict owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SHA-256 assignment fingerprint, three-slot hard cap, read-only tool restrictions, event/report transport and parent tool selection. - Closure path: rejected duplicate submission returns an error to the parent before a second child exists; individual child reports return to the parent for task use. No observed or structurally wired conflict between existing S1s is tracked/attenuated with a subsequent worker-response loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: preventing a duplicate request at the tool interface and serializing receipt of results do not demonstrate regulation of interactions among already operative S1 cells. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents/manager.ts; src/agent/subagents/tools.ts; src/agent/subagents/worker.ts; src/tools/definitions/subagents.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: manage current, parallel operational investigation commitments and capacity under one active coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing child research assignments, unfinished or irrelevant investigations, cancellation/reprioritization needs, saturation of the three-worker capacity and partial/incomplete reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main model views active child roster/status/results, chooses distinct assignments to start, may stop/correct/restart tasks, await any completed child, and integrates verified findings into subsequent main work. - Decision owner: first-party model-directed main agent as current controller over several distinct read-only operational S1 cells; SubagentManager enforces commands and capacity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-scoped child map, list/read/wait/stop/restart tool API, abort controllers, per-child event/report state, shared model system guidance and maximum-concurrency guard. - Closure path: whole-session current child states and pending research needs → parent model start/stop/restart/dependency decision → SubagentManager action on target child → updated status/return report → parent model subsequent assignment/implementation choices. - Boundary reachability: src/agent/runner.ts includes orchestration guidance in first-party agent context, src/agent/turn/tool-execution/single-tool.ts dispatches runSubagentTool, and manager.ts supplies live child control; enabled orchestration mode is a shipped operator-supported mode rather than an unconnected API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: holding the manager&#39;s maps and hard cap fixed without parent model decisions would not select appropriate parallel research scopes, react to different child results, or redirect current effort with task discretion. - Whole-system current view: active session&#39;s primary task and each running, stopping or completed child investigation, identified assignments, last events/results and available capacity; not global company or other users&#39; sessions. - Current-control decision scope: assign independent evidence tasks, allocate up to three child work cells, stop obsolete work, restart with revised instructions, await and use outcomes to reprioritize current coding investigation. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents/manager.ts; src/agent/subagents/tools.ts; src/tools/definitions/subagents.ts; src/agent/runner.ts; src/agent/turn/tool-execution/single-tool.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: credited only in enabled multi-investigation mode, not every default single-task execution. No free-standing automated watchdog, multiwriter fleet or parent-governed (P) mode asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no distinct built-in independent audit of execution outcomes with authoritative corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: wrong code, unjustified success claims, missed tests and inaccurate tool/evidence reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main task agent runs tools, interprets test results and verifies claims; optionally delegated read-only workers deliver research reports whose instructions explicitly say the parent verifies them, rather than an independent auditor passing judgment over an operational claim. - Decision owner: primary agent for normal self-checking and report use; no materially distinct S3 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evidence governor, progress confirmation, terminal/test outputs, read-only context gatherers, post-task plan status and human scope approvals. - Closure path: the original S1 interprets evidence and may continue; no separate reviewer opinion about completed S1 output is wired to corrective control independently of the originating task actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed investigator children are real, but they are general delegated research operations; they do not automatically constitute the complementary audit function merely by reading files. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents/worker.ts; src/agent/subagents/tools.ts; src/agent/evidence-governor.ts; src/agent/task-evidence.ts; src/agent/runner.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed organization-level outside/future intelligence/option-development path beyond current task investigation and provider resilience. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environmental/tool changes or project direction might require capability adaptation, but the supported agent loop is goal/current-task oriented. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent can investigate present questions, user may switch providers/skills, system rotates keys when failing, and task history persists; none is a demonstrated future capability adoption decision. - Decision owner: task-level model for tactical research, deterministic fallback for current provider failure, operator for tools/skills settings; no distinct S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider fallback/rotation, memory/compaction, workspace-context summaries, read-only investigations, skill discovery and existing-session continuation. - Closure path: present facts or fallback errors change next task action/model selection, not a future/environmental adaptation option selected and returned to durable system capability or whole-current S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tactical research and resource recovery serve active S1 rather than shifting the organization&#39;s future strategy/capability. - Evidence: src/agent/runner.ts; src/agent/subagents/worker.ts; src/agent/context-manager.ts; src/agent/task-analyzer.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate decision/return channel for constitutional project identity, purpose or ultimate policy of the installed agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe shell actions, out-of-scope pentest targets, provider/payment preferences and consent to destructive tools are real operational constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator specifies engagement scope and risk/approval limits; first-party controls enforce those limits on tool invocations rather than resolving organization-identity disputes. - Decision owner: human user/config for scope/consent and deterministic runtime guards for validation; no first-party ultimate-policy S5 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scope-based target allowlists/denylists, rate/concurrency caps, DNS/redirect checking, safe/confirm/block rules, cost/free-only configuration and user confirmation prompts. - Closure path: tool action allowed/denied/confirmed changes its execution; no identity/policy issue escalates to a legitimate parent who settles purpose and returns an organization-wide governing decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may propose a task outside allowed scope, but cannot legitimately redefine the operator&#39;s ultimate constitutional rules through approval/tool output. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent/session-policy.ts; src/agent/turn/tool-execution/authorization.ts; src/agent/turn/tool-execution/gates.ts; src/agent/scope-context.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6fc704c99e84a88d45b66dad0cc208f97cb53933/assessments/clai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6fc704c99e84a88d45b66dad0cc208f97cb53933\">6fc704c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T12:57:56Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tangle-network/agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tangle Agent Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tangle-network/agent-runtime/tree/ed008c7623362720c47b1e628dbc19073e196b91\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed008c7623362720c47b1e628dbc19073e196b91\">ed008c7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform delegated task work through agentic worker loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task state, tool/environment results, failures, missing information, and backend/provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions from current task observations. - Decision owner: executing model-driven worker agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker executors/backends, Agent.act, tool/harness integration, scope lifecycle, retries, accounting, and settlement. - Closure path: assigned task → worker inference/action → environment/tool result → later worker decision → operational output. - Boundary reachability: workers are the normal execution units of shipped supervise/Supervisor paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host code constrains and executes actions, while model cognition owns substantive next-action choice. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; src/runtime/supervise/types.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider-side cognition remains external; credit is for the first-party execution loop around it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate resource contention among concurrently active child S1 units sharing finite execution capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent children can jointly request more tokens, USD, iterations, named resources, or live-worker slots than the parent can commit safely. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently executing child agents in one recursive supervisor tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous child reservations contend for one finite parent-owned resource pool; uncontrolled admission could overcommit capacity. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party BudgetPool reserves enforced channels atomically, rejects insufficient admission, reconciles actual spend, refunds known unused capacity, and restores reservations conservatively after recovery. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: typed spawn rejection/shortfall and changed free capacity return into the live manager path and affect later spawn/work choices. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a concrete cross-worker finite-resource interference plus an atomic attenuation mechanism, not messaging or delegation alone. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runtime deterministically decides whether a requested reservation fits; a composed manager chooses what to do after refusal or changed capacity. - Decision owner: no autonomous S2 owner is established by the deterministic reservation mechanism itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conserved budget pool, atomic reservation/reconciliation, root-wide worker cap, fail-closed unknown measurements, typed spawn rejections/shortfalls. - Closure path: competing child commitments → reserve/reject atomically → capacity/refusal visible to manager → subsequent work choices operate under reconciled capacity. - Boundary reachability: budget admission is part of shipped recursive Scope/supervise execution across nested children. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2-specific mechanism is first-party, but its collision arbitration is deterministic rather than autonomous, so constructor credit is used. - Evidence: src/runtime/supervise/budget.ts; src/runtime/supervise/scope.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic shared budgets are not credited by themselves; credit rests on finite cross-child contention and fail-closed admission/reconciliation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a current view of the supervised tree and exercise present-tense authority over commitments, steering, cancellation, and further allocation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active workers can stall, fail, drift, need redirection, finish asynchronously, or leave the parent objective incomplete while capacity changes. - Whole-system current view: one authoritative Scope exposes the live child set, progress/events, settlements, spend/reservations, and recursively nested execution state to the manager path. - Current-control decision scope: spawn, await/observe, steer, stop/cancel, react to completion checks, and choose the next move under current resource/depth/concurrency limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which worker commitments to create, continue, redirect, or terminate and when the objective is complete enough to stop. - Decision owner: model-driven supervisor/driver agent in the shipped supervise/coordination path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scope, Supervisor, coordination tools, journals/recovery, progress/trace access, budgets, worker caps, cancellation, and completion gating. - Closure path: live worker/resource state → supervisor model decision → runtime applies spawn/steer/stop/etc. → changed tree state returns through the same scope. - Boundary reachability: public supervise(profile, task, opts) is explicitly a manager agent driving workers under one budget. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic machinery enforces constraints, while the supervisor model chooses dynamic topology/intervention at runtime. - Evidence: README.md; docs/canonical-api.md; src/runtime/supervise/scope.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this positive state is specific to the supported model-driven supervisor mode, not every fixed topology/combinator.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: check operational claims/results through evidence paths that do not rely on the producing worker&#39;s own success assertion and return findings into control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can claim completion despite a wrong result, or ordinary worker reporting can omit failures visible in direct output/trace evidence. - Claim being audited: whether worker output satisfies the parent deliverable and, for analyst routes, what failures/patterns appear in worker tool-trace evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: worker settlement/final output and normal progress/results returned to the parent. - Complementary access path: parent completion checks execute against actual worker output; trace analysts can read structured worker trace evidence and publish findings separately from worker prose. - Independence boundary: check/analyst execution is outside the producing worker self-report path; the external final judge stays write-only/outside steering and is not borrowed as an internal owner. - Who acts on findings: supervisor/driver consumes failed completion or routed analyst findings and can revise acceptance/continuation/steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runtime supplies independent evidence/check/finding-return structure; deployment supplies substantive check/analyst semantics in the general case. - Decision owner: no guaranteed autonomous first-party auditor exists in every base deployment; a composed verifier/analyst can own the judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: completion gate, verifier/check APIs, trace evidence, analyst routes, finding events, and judge-to-steering firewall. - Closure path: worker output/trace → independent check/analyst path → pass/fail/finding → supervisor changes acceptance/continuation/control. - Boundary reachability: completion-gated delivery and analyst coordination mechanisms are shipped runtime surfaces, while substantive audit configuration remains composition-dependent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party constructor closes evidence and feedback paths but not a mandatory autonomous auditor, so constructor credit is used. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; src/mcp/tools/coordination.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external benchmark judge is intentionally excluded from the execution-control tree and is not used to inflate this state.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: construct and independently measure candidate changes to agent/profile/code surfaces for later operation while separating search from final assessment and activation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current configuration may underperform on fresh/future work; search may overfit or mutate live state before independent validation. - External distinction: improvement consumes caller-supplied scenarios/objectives and can use production/search findings about environment/domain performance outside the current execution tree. - Future / prospective distinction: train/selection/final-test separation asks whether a changed surface should improve subsequent runs rather than merely finish the current action. - Adaptation option generated: optimization methods/candidate generators produce changed profile/code/tool/MCP/memory surfaces with exact candidate identity/diffs. - Path back into current capability / S3: selected candidates are independently final-tested, bound into review/activation records, and can become later runtime state after an authorized application-owned atomic write. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runtime owns candidate identity, measurement binding, final-test isolation, review/activation records, and result validation; caller/application owns domain objective/execution and final adoption write. - Decision owner: no autonomous first-party S4 actor closes the complete domain sensing→adaptation→adoption loop alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: improve, optimization-method integration, partition separation, candidate diffs/population, proposal review, activation identity/expiry/retry validation, application transaction port. - Closure path: external/fresh evidence → candidate generation/search → independent final-test comparison → review/activation → application write → later runtime uses changed capability. - Boundary reachability: these are public runtime APIs, but documentation explicitly preserves caller/application composition boundaries. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the adaptation constructor is first-party and function-specific, while decisive domain adaptation/adoption remains composition-dependent. - Evidence: docs/improve.md; docs/learning-flywheel.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: learning documentation explicitly says the complete process is not itself a demonstrated standalone learner; constructor credit does not assume that closure.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no qualifying first-party identity / ultimate-policy function established within the assessed runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity-level or constitutional-policy disturbance is not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying ultimate-policy decision right is established. - Decision owner: none identified for an S5 function inside the assessed operating boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: profiles, permissions, resource constraints, human review, and activation authorization exist but are execution/adaptation controls rather than an identity organ. - Closure path: no path was found from an identity/ultimate-policy issue through legitimate ultimate authority and back into subsequent runtime operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: because no qualifying S5 function is established, autonomy is not graded. - Evidence: docs/improve.md; docs/architecture.md. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary human approvals, profile configuration, and repository governance are not treated as S5 by substitution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/73639464547d22d8b6764245e02810b8ffa46601/assessments/tangle-agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 73639464547d22d8b6764245e02810b8ffa46601\">7363946 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T19:10:04Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/henrik-me/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Henrik Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/henrik-me/agent-harness/tree/9168fa451b69c5d53b961ab5c805b3067597ade4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9168fa451b69c5d53b961ab5c805b3067597ade4\">9168fa4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce substantive bounded changes and reviews in consumer software work through distinct model-backed contributors, and source-connected Copilot PR analysis. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-specific source/edit choices, unexpected actual code state, review findings and failure/repair feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the supported model-operated consumer mode, each contributor model chooses content of its scoped implementation or critique; GitHub Copilot chooses PR review findings from the actual current PR head. - Decision owner: Externally provided model agents acting under first-party scoped briefing and PR-review invocation; human/operator chooses the task and final merge, not the agent&#39;s substantive code/review response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness dispatch emitted canonical prompt and owned-file scope, WORKBOARD.md, clickstop plan, real GitHub PRs, reviewer model allowlists and managed review gate checks. - Closure path: Consumer work/PR requirement → orchestrator supplies bounded first-party briefing or CLI requests Copilot reviewer → model performs implementation or PR review → changed files/review findings returned to the consumer PR/workboard → further correction and submission. - Boundary reachability: harness init/sync installs the model-enabled process instructions; harness review explicitly performs the GitHub reviewer request/poll rather than only drawing an agent diagram. External agent host/Copilot authorization must be configured; CLI-only mode has no model executor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed specialists and reviewer choose substantive decisions; deterministic Node tooling cannot derive their implementation or review findings without the model. This is an A mode only in the declared connected model-operated consumer cell, not for a standalone Node CLI. - Evidence: model roles and parallel work, subagent briefing and output, review execution path, real Copilot review request/poll. - Basis: First-party consumer operating contract plus executable external model reviewer integration. - Confidence: Medium source-structural; distinct implementer execution depends on external agent host not bundled. - Caveats: GitHub reviewer credentials, model quality, independent coding success and a complete live adopter project were not demonstrated. No autonomous execution claimed for the CLI itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent destructive conflicts among parallel operational contributors and overlapping currently active work claims before they affect other S1 outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent specialists silently overwriting one another&#39;s files and two orchestrator work claims racing for an already occupied coordination slot. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Establish disjoint per-agent editable file scopes and disallow a second active work claim for one owner unless deliberately transferred/taken over. - Decision owner: The parent orchestrator or operator decides actual task partition/scopes and takeovers; first-party deterministic CLI state and briefing constructors enforce/show preconditions, not autonomous mutual compromise. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit owned/read-only/do-not-touch briefing fields, no-commit worker discipline, WORKBOARD ownership rows, apply-time re-read/refusal, scoped clickstop branches. - Closure path: Parent receives parallel task candidates → declares separate file ownership in each emitted brief and claims a single active work slot → worker follows scoped briefing; conflicting claim is rejected, requiring reassignment before additional work proceeds → subsequent agent tasks operate on revised or nonoverlapping assignments. - Boundary reachability: harness dispatch builds emitted own/do-not-touch sections on every configured dispatch; claim directly checks the current WORKBOARD at plan and mutation time and fails closed for active owner collisions. These are delivered consumer-facing CLI commands and managed policy, not an aspirational slide. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concrete inter-worker lost-edit failure is documented in the source policy, and S2-specific interference is regulated through source-wired constructor/enforcement. The external orchestrator must pick the compromise, and the CLI does not independently detect all overlaps between distinct work files/agents. - Distinct S1 units: Independently model-operated specialist implementers assigned different concurrently active clickstop work and file scopes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two contributors can write one overlapping file and silently lose the earlier changes; the process source describes an observed lib/templating.mjs / lib/lock.mjs collision from parallel dispatch. - Attenuating coordination relation: Allocate exclusive writable sets per specialist and a single active work claim per owner; reject owner collision at apply-time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A conflicting owner work claim fails or a shared file becomes explicitly read-only for one worker; parent must rebrief/serialize/reclaim before launching the affected worker. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: These targeted mechanisms mitigate a stated inter-contributor lost-edit race and concurrent work-claim collision. Generic passing of a task ID or state alone is not counted. - Evidence: actual lost-edit incident and explicit file ownership, briefing file scope, claim collision check, apply-time recheck. - Basis: Shipped S2-specific coordination constructor with source-documented disturbance. - Confidence: Medium-high for the narrow fail-closed work-claim check and explicit overlapping-file procedure. - Caveats: The emitted brief does not compare all concurrent file scope declarations mechanically; adherence depends on the actual external orchestrator/worker.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Assemble a whole-current delivery view and a bounded work-commitment lifecycle, allowing an external management actor to choose and revise active staffing, priorities and release/rework intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing active clickstops, stale work, incomplete handoffs, unidentified worker ownership, current blocked dependencies and inconsistent PR closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which planned clickstop to activate, assign its owner/branch, accept a failed review and instruct rework, decide whether to close/release completed work. - Decision owner: The source-prescribed external orchestrator or responsible human in the consumer project. The Node CLI gathers/checks workboard/current-state evidence and applies selected transitions; it does not autonomously choose among priority alternatives. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WORKBOARD, harness status, harness claim, harness closeout, progress reports, single-active-work gate, review verdicts and CI merge controls. - Closure path: Whole current WORKBOARD and planned clickstop inventory → external manager identifies a current commitment, invokes claim/dispatch → workers return status/review evidence → manager decides correction/reallocation or closeout → first-party CLI updates active work and subsequent assignment context. - Boundary reachability: Consumer installation materializes the workboard and process docs; shipped claim/status/closeout commands read/write actual files and refuse inconsistent transitions. The core Node tool is a constructor for current-control decisions; the source does not auto-launch the orchestrator model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-coded management acts only when operated by an external agent host; removing that host leaves deterministic workboard plumbing but no discretionary portfolio negotiation. A positive C here identifies a specific delivered S3 control constructor, not automatic S3=A or generic workflow completeness. - Whole-system current view: The active/planned clickstop table and owner/branch/blocked metadata in WORKBOARD plus PR/review state and reporting, at the one consumer delivery-cell recursion. - Current-control decision scope: Active CS allocation/owner, constrained parallelism, failed review return and closeout of a project&#39;s current delivery commitments, not host-company staffing or long-term constitutional goals. - Evidence: claim current-cell view, claim mutation, closeout, orchestrator management role. - Basis: Specific first-party current-control observation, transition and return machinery, with externally owned discretionary S3 right. - Confidence: Medium source-structural. - Caveats: Human/LLM consumer actor is required; no standing self-running S3 agent, whole-system budget negotiator or parent-governed S3 (P) path is established by this source. Workboard alone does not count; the positive constructor depends on its wired claim/rework/closeout loop.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently inspect producer claims against the actual PR head and return findings that alter acceptance/rework decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementer overconfidence, stale review against an old commit, omitted blocking code defects or approval from a reviewer sharing the implementer&#39;s model context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-based reviewer chooses a fresh Go/Needs-Fix and issue findings from reviewed PR evidence, independently of the producing actor; first-party review framework rejects reviewer-model conflicts and refuses to treat blocking findings as approval. - Decision owner: Independently invoked reviewer model (local rubber-duck when supplied, or GitHub Copilot PR reviewer) for factual/technical judgment, with first-party CI acting as enforcement. A model that also implemented the PR is barred by the configured separation rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: assertReviewerAllowed, model audit fields, actual PR HEAD and diff evidence, reviewer prompt, Copilot REST reviewer invocation, per-HEAD review log and managed review-gates.yml required checks. - Closure path: Implementer submits current source/PR → independent model reviews that head and identifies findings → computeVerdict returns No-Go/Needs-Fix on blocking output, logs exact reviewed SHA → review workflow blocks integration until a valid current-head review → orchestrator/worker reworks PR and requests a new round. - Boundary reachability: harness review first-party command genuinely requests Copilot reviewer through GitHub when review config enables it; review-gates.yml is shipped to consumers via init/sync and includes independent/attached evidence checks; source documents the reviewer dispatch as the normal content-PR gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer model assesses producer output from separately obtained actual PR source, chooses critique and can force new work; deterministic log parsing and CI do not themselves own that independent judgment. - Claim being audited: Current implementer/subagent work is correct and ready to merge under its original clickstop acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker completion report and PR author statements, branch status and routine task tests. - Complementary access path: Separate model reviewer analyzes actual PR current head/diff and required reading, with Copilot&#39;s independently attached GitHub review as a distinct channel. - Independence boundary: Different reviewer model from recorded implementers is enforced for local audit; Copilot is a separate GitHub review actor; primary evidence and head SHA are checked instead of recycling author summary alone. - Who acts on findings: First-party verdict and required checks withhold Go/merge evidence; supervised orchestrator triggers repairs and a new review round. - Evidence: review PR and independent-model gate, Copilot review and combined verdict, verdict logic, Copilot actual review, managed enforcement. - Basis: Explicit source-connected complementary reviewer judgment, distinct access and rework/block closure path. - Confidence: Medium-high for configured reviewer-enabled consumer mode; actual real PR review not replayed in this isolated assessment. - Caveats: Without GitHub authentication, enabled review checks and a responding reviewer, the model audit is not executable; source review is not a measured guarantee that Copilot detects all errors. Optional manual-only mode should not be conflated with autonomous reviewer operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Scan external changes with a prospective model, develop options for changing future delivery-cell capability and return chosen adaptation to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in future tooling, supported environments, dependencies or customer delivery needs requiring a changed organizational capability rather than one repaired work item. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped first-party externally prospective adaptation judgment and closed option-to-capability feedback right found. - Decision owner: Not established: harness upgrade compares a user-specified target Git ref deterministically, while harvest lists stale existing learning entries for later operator disposition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Upgrade dry-run diff, version pin and managed sync, LEARNINGS.md harvest, clickstop planning, changelog and maintenance rules. - Closure path: No first-party model of external future conditions that selects among capability change options, returns the prospective decision to S3, and updates later operational capacity without an externally authored plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Upgrade preview and manual version application can change installed files, but the candidate ref and adoption judgment are supplied from outside, not selected through a shipped strategic prospective loop; stale-learning surfacing is retrospective operational improvement. - Evidence: upgrade takes specified ref and previews only, harvest stale items, model/role process configuration. - Basis: Bounded negative at first-party installed/CLI paths, not universal impossibility for custom consumers. - Confidence: Medium; human-managed downstream process evolution can be built from these primitives but is not itself source-closed S4. - Caveats: Consumer humans may perform strategic adaptation; the source does not warrant crediting that unobserved higher-recursion function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve legitimate ultimate-policy/identity disputes over the consumer delivery cell&#39;s enduring mission and reconcile current versus future orientations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting enduring purposes, permissible changes of identity, constitutional operating constraints or ultimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established by the first-party shipped process. User approval gates concern task-plan/implementation details or acceptance of a code/review change, not a reconstructable constitutional adjudication path. - Decision owner: No source-connected S5 ultimate-policy actor is evidenced; the adopter or project owner remains external higher recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: INSTRUCTIONS, CODEOFCONDUCT, CONVENTIONS, review rulesets, workboard approval policy, optional owner override, credentials and repository settings. - Closure path: No identity-level issue → legitimate constitutional authority → authoritative decision → re-governed ongoing operations path established in first-party artifact. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Managed policy files and approvals constrain delivery, but do not transform their configured owners into a standing S5 entity. Model roles cannot independently rewrite legitimate identity. - Evidence: operating approval context, review policy, workboard gate. - Basis: Bounded negative following inspection of permission, process governance and approval mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium-high in this assessed recursion. - Caveats: A consumer can attach genuine external S5 institution; it is not first-party authority supplied by the library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/12eb45991ddd86b008d65053dbcf2dcfbce61ac3/assessments/he/henrik-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 12eb45991ddd86b008d65053dbcf2dcfbce61ac3\">12eb459 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T23:59:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/prasenjeet-symon/ogcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ogcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/prasenjeet-symon/ogcode/tree/b9077a0fcca60195da4426b3f733933b533be705\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b9077a0fcca60195da4426b3f733933b533be705\">b9077a0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce repository-facing software outcomes through open-ended model/tool coding action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous code structure, incomplete implementation details, syntax/build/test failures, repository state, tool output, external documentation, context limits and changing user requirements encountered during implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what project evidence to inspect, which coding/tool action to take, how to respond to returned failures/evidence, what implementation satisfies the assigned outcome and when local work is complete. - Decision owner: model-backed BuildAgent in interactive work and model-backed TaskAgent in each plan worktree. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry; repository maps/search/read/edit/write/bash; syntax checks; provider adapters; permissions; sessions; compaction/memory; worktrees and git; cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: software goal/task description plus project context → agent selects action/tool → tool/environment result returns into the same agent loop → agent revises implementation or verifies/finishes → repository-facing result is produced. - Boundary reachability: BuildAgent is the normal interactive mode; TaskAgent is constructed by the standard plan/task execution path and runs the shipped coding loop in its worktree without adopter-authored orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed Build/Task actor while retaining tools, worktrees, stores and permission machinery removes the task-specific software-engineering judgment. - Evidence: internal/agent/agent.go; internal/agent/loop.go; internal/server/taskroutes.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model inference and GitHub remote service behavior are dependencies; the credited loop/control composition is first-party Ogcode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent destructive interference and merge conflict among parallel implementation S1 workstreams by deciding file ownership/dependency relations before execution and feeding those relations into task scope/order. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently executing TaskAgents can otherwise modify overlapping files from parallel branches, creating incompatible patches/merge conflicts; dependent implementation can also run before prerequisite changes are present. - Distinct S1 units: separate TaskAgent sessions executing different implementation tasks in distinct git worktrees/branches. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping parallel file mutation can produce competing patches at integration time; premature dependent execution can act on a repository state missing predecessor work. - Attenuating coordination relation: BreakdownAgent must assign each file to exactly one parallel workstream and add a dependency whenever two tasks need the same file. It also defines linear dependencies reflecting implementation prerequisites. The runtime then isolates tasks in worktrees and executes dependencies only after predecessor integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: BreakdownAgent&#39;s task descriptions/dependencies become the authoritative TaskAgent prompts and task records; the chosen graph determines whether a task starts concurrently or waits, which branch/worktree it sees, and which files it is instructed to modify. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the first-party breakdown prompt explicitly names the concrete shared-file merge-conflict disturbance and requires file ownership/dependency decisions to attenuate it. Worktrees and dependency scheduling enforce that selected coordination relation rather than merely moving work between agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the task-specific partition of files/workstreams and the dependency relation needed to avoid cross-worker conflict while preserving safe parallelism. - Decision owner: model-backed BreakdownAgent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured submittaskbreakdown; task dependency records; per-task worktrees/branches; chain branches; dependency readiness checks; atomic task claims; serialized git metadata mutation; automatic dependent startup after predecessor integration. - Closure path: locked plan/repository evidence → BreakdownAgent identifies scopes/dependencies under explicit anti-conflict rules → structured task graph is stored → server creates isolated worktrees and schedules according to that graph → TaskAgents receive scoped descriptions and predecessor state → later S1 operation follows the selected coordination. - Boundary reachability: BreakdownAgent, breakdown tool and plan/task materialization are invoked automatically by the standard locked-plan mode; no consumer-authored coordinator is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing BreakdownAgent leaves deterministic mechanisms capable of enforcing a supplied graph, but removes the discretionary, task-specific choice of which files/workstreams should be parallel versus ordered. - Evidence: internal/agent/breakdown.go; internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tool/breakdown.go; internal/server/taskroutes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: worktree/dependency enforcement itself is deterministic; autonomy is assigned to the BreakdownAgent&#39;s organizational partition decision, not to git or the scheduler.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: give the legitimate operator a whole-plan current view and authority to intervene in active implementation commitments on behalf of the current software-delivery whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plan may contain pending, blocked, running, failed and completed tasks; a current TaskAgent can stall, take a wrong path, fail, need retry or need operator-directed interruption while other plan commitments continue. - Whole-system current view: the first-party task board groups every task in the active plan by current status, shows total/done/running/failed/pending counts, dependency eligibility and per-task branch/session/PR state. - Current-control decision scope: start one eligible task or all eligible tasks; inspect a running task&#39;s live agent session; abort that running session; mark current work complete/failed where appropriate; retry failed work from a clean task state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which currently eligible commitments are started and when a current task session should be interrupted/retried/failed as part of supervising the active plan. - Decision owner: human/operator at the parent recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan/task stores; task-board projections; task/session routes; server running-loop registry; context cancellation; dependency scheduler; worktree cleanup/retry; SSE/bus status updates. - Closure path: task board/current session exposes whole-plan current state → operator selects start/intervention/retry or abort → first-party API mutates task state or cancels the registered task session loop → task/plan events and dependency eligibility update → subsequent current execution changes. - Boundary reachability: the browser plan/task board and task-execution session are standard product surfaces; TaskAgent loops are registered in the same server running map consumed by the generic first-party session-abort endpoint. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BreakdownAgent selects the initial coordination graph but does not receive a live whole-plan control loop. Runtime auto-start is deterministic enforcement of the frozen dependency graph. The discretionary current-control mode established in the frozen product belongs to the operator. - Evidence: web/src/pages/plan-tasks.tsx; web/src/pages/task-execution.tsx; internal/server/taskroutes.go; internal/server/sessionroutes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a parent/operator supervisory mode, not autonomous S3. The deterministic dependency scheduler and automatic completion transitions do not themselves own discretionary whole-system current-control judgment.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit loop was established that independently challenges implementation claims and returns a distinct audit judgment into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation agents can be wrong about the correctness/completeness of their own changes, but the frozen standard distribution does not package a separate reviewer/evaluator role or audit gate with complementary evidence access. - Claim being audited: no standard first-party independent-audit claim/route was established. - Ordinary reporting path: BuildAgent/TaskAgent implement changes and are themselves instructed to run syntax/build/test/lint verification before declaring completion. - Complementary access path: none established as an audit function. The generic task tool can spawn a clean-context read-only investigator over code/web evidence, but its shipped contract is broad investigation/context offload rather than independent review of producer claims, and no standard reviewer role/accept-correct loop is wired around task completion. - Independence boundary: no material audit boundary is established beyond optional generic investigation. - Who acts on findings: no standard independent-audit finding/closure path established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: self-verification in coding prompts; read-only TaskTool investigation; syntax checker; builds/tests; git/PR artifacts. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard complementary auditor judgment is returned as a distinct control input. - Boundary reachability: not applicable for a positive path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary tests and the implementer&#39;s own verification are the production path, while generic clean-context investigation is not packaged as an audit function in the frozen distribution. - Evidence: internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tool/task.go; internal/server/taskroutes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator or BuildAgent could formulate a review-like prompt for the generic read-only child, but generic subagent expressiveness is not treated as a standard-distribution S3 audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then loop was established that senses external/future change, develops organizational adaptation options and returns a selected adaptation into present capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation/search, prior project decisions, model/tool availability and persistent memory can influence current software work, but none forms a first-party prospective adaptation cycle at the selected organization boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deepsearch and page fetching; PlanAgent; NoteAgent; session/project memory recall; MEMORY.md; skills/MCP/model configuration; compaction; persistent sessions and archived plans. - Closure path: not applicable; no external/future distinction → adaptation-option development → selected persistent capability/strategy change → return to current S3/capability loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: PlanAgent plans current requested software, recall restores past operational context, and search answers present questions. These paths do not establish prospective organizational adaptation merely because they contain research, learning or persistence. - Evidence: README.md; internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tool/memoryrecall.go; internal/tool/projectmemoryrecall.go; internal/agent/memorymd.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the README contains a broader future computer-agent vision; intake explicitly excludes those roadmap claims from current-state evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: carry legitimate project-owner standing instructions/rules into every relevant Ogcode agent context so later software work remains governed by project-level policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding/planning/task agents can otherwise diverge from repository architecture, conventions, risk boundaries or durable owner instructions across sessions/worktrees. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which durable project rules, priorities and behavioral constraints legitimately govern Ogcode&#39;s work in the selected repository. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Parent (P) — the legitimate project owner/editor owns project AGENTS.md / AGENT.md instruction content. No autonomous agent-owned identity/ultimate-policy mode is established. - Return-to-operation path: Ogcode discovers owner-authored instruction files from root to current project directory, preserves root-to-leaf precedence, loads their text into the agent system-prompt construction and thereby governs later Build/Plan/Task execution in that project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the durable project-level instructions and precedence that should constrain future agent operation. - Decision owner: human/project owner at the parent recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: instruction-file discovery/loading; root-to-leaf ordering; prompt block rendering/escaping; system prompt builder; ordinary file/version-control persistence. - Closure path: project-level policy issue → legitimate owner changes AGENTS.md/AGENT.md → Ogcode reloads project instructions for agent execution → instructions enter system context → subsequent software operation is governed by the returned policy. - Boundary reachability: AGENTS.md/AGENT.md loading is first-party runtime code invoked by the standard agent prompt construction; it is not a contributor-only plugin or external wrapper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents consume the standing instructions but do not receive a first-party mechanism making their self-authored policy legitimate ultimate authority; ownership remains with the project authority that supplies the files. - Evidence: internal/agent/agentmd.go; internal/agent/loop.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permission prompts and ordinary task/plan approval are operational controls and are not used as S5 evidence; S5 is limited to durable project-recursion policy returned through the project instruction surface.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/84c26bb06c83f644c72a156d8214cfcb5a19bcf8/assessments/ogcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 84c26bb06c83f644c72a156d8214cfcb5a19bcf8\">84c26bb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:12:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/proliferate-ai/proliferate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Proliferate</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-16\">2026-09-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/proliferate-ai/proliferate/tree/74e1178cf3ecfbfc3ffeb8321f82c4a9bfce620f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 74e1178cf3ecfbfc3ffeb8321f82c4a9bfce620f\">74e1178 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: standard product sessions run native coding-agent harnesses that autonomously inspect, edit and execute work inside bounded task workspaces; child sessions can independently complete delegated scoped work and return results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: first-party isolation, parent→child messaging and exact completion wakeups are conflict avoidance/delegation mechanisms. The pinned implementation does not provide an agent-owned mutual-adjustment loop that observes and dampens interference or oscillation among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the product control plane provisions workspaces, sessions and workflows, while a parent may supervise its own delegated children, but no distinct autonomous actor is given current-whole authority to regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities across the operational system. Scheduling/provisioning alone is not Beer S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the first-party review runtime establishes an independent audit path. Review-only agents inspect trusted plan/code targets, are prohibited from modifying them, submit structured pass/critique results, and return findings to the parent session. The runtime can automatically iterate parent revision → trusted recapture → fresh review round until pass or a configured round limit. This is a ready, agent-enacted complementary audit/feedback loop rather than a developer-supplied verifier primitive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: recurring/event-driven workflows react to configured events and can run tasks such as triage or dependency updates, but the pinned distribution does not establish a persistent agent-owned external/prospective intelligence function that models environmental change and feeds adaptation into present control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: harness choice, workflow definitions, reviewer configuration, permissions and review policy remain product/operator configuration. No first-party agent holds final authority over organizational identity, purpose or constitutional policy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ff74c380425fb7c8a9cee512289cdb428d74dbb1/assessments/proliferate.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ff74c380425fb7c8a9cee512289cdb428d74dbb1\">ff74c38 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T04:20:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Darwin-Agent/HarnessX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HarnessX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Darwin-Agent/HarnessX/tree/915520584c92cc35ed24aef1af17eec1d60c0b01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 915520584c92cc35ed24aef1af17eec1d60c0b01\">9155205 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Conduct current user/evaluation task work by selecting available tools through the task agent and adapting next model actions after real tool execution observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New task/environment inputs, failing or successful real tools, edits/observations, unexpected model answers, session interruptions and capability budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured model produces tool calls and subsequent decisions; source-owned RunLoop executes registered tool callbacks and inserts real results into task state/conversation; this changes next possible actions and the produced task result. - Decision owner: First-party HarnessX installed model-backed task agent using third-party inference through an explicit provider boundary. Tool registry/hooks and model budgets limit discretionary task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessConfig, RunLoop, tool registry and ToolResultEvent, state/trajectory, hook processors, workspace and sandbox, cost guard and interruption/resume. - Closure path: Task → Harness.run → model-driven RunLoop → selected registered tool call → executable handler → ToolResultEvent appended to state → next model turn/action/final deliverable → task trajectory, used by evolution. - Boundary reachability: Shipped public Harness.run and provider configuration lead directly to runloop with actual tool registry execution and observation persistence. This is source-level production code, not just an interface or benchmark score. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool choice and subsequent result-informed task continuation belong to the configured model-backed agent; process hooks and static restrictions are safeguards and do not stand in for autonomous task decisions. - Evidence: harnessx/core/harness.py; harnessx/core/harness.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py. - Basis: First-party model/tool/result closure in shipped operational runtime. - Confidence: High structural confidence; no external provider inference executed. - Caveats: Deterministic processor execution without a configured model is not by itself S1=A. External benchmark correctness is unverified.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party disturbance-specific reciprocal coordination role across independently viable S1 operating units is established at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel evaluation tasks or agent subprocesses may share budgets, files and outputs; runtime processors and meta-agent write-scope gates constrain local conflicts rather than observing competing viable units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tool call/processor ordering, task timeout, separate round/config workspaces, and write-path gates block or sequence current work. There is no shipped S2 arbitration decision choosing mutual adjustments between independent S1 units from sensed interference. - Decision owner: Per-task RunLoop and static plugin/recipe configuration, external operator for parallelism; no legitimate standing S2 controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Processor pipeline, workspace/sandbox isolation, write/read scopes, run/round files and configured evaluation concurrency. - Closure path: Tool tasks/rounds → isolated/sequenced execution → results; missing identified inter-S1 operational disturbance → agent-selected damping policy → returned reciprocal operating restrictions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker scheduling, output isolation and sandbox hardening are infrastructure, not an evidenced VSM S2-specific inter-unit interference regulator. - Evidence: harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/core/runloop.py; harnessx/metaharness/processors/writescopegate.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; harnessx/core/processor.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing parallel tasks, hooks, meta-agent workspace/write conflict surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high for frozen first-party sample modes. - Caveats: A multi-tenant adopter could use plugins to implement such policies; hypothetical external implementations do not count.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No owner makes binding whole-current priority/resource/commitment choices over separate ongoing viable S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed evaluations, incomplete rounds, step/cost limits and rejected harness edits affect current experiment progress; no multi-division current operating commitments are modeled. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Recipe controller selects fixed benchmark task sets and configured round order; meta-agent proposes harness redesign, the validator rejects invalid candidates, and acceptance applies to future model-agent configs. There is no first-party global portfolio operating priority decision with S1 management intervention. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and code-configured round runner; local agent and meta-agent for task/design choices, not an accountable whole-current S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Round state, currentconfig, task eval logs, budgets, postflight validation, recovery/fallback for the experiment. - Closure path: Configured run → eval and improvement → next round; missing whole-current multi-unit status → legitimate obligations/resource tradeoff decision → binding cross-unit current action return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Meta-agent evolves capability across rounds (S4 here), rather than holding a standing current-control mandate for multiple separate viable S1 operating cells. - Evidence: recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/gaiaevolver/run.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py. - Basis: Tested meta-agent, benchmark controller and task manager candidates against S3 scope. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Human research team/resource management or downstream customer program control are not in the first-party running harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently sourced complementary audit of a standing S3&#39;s operational claims with returned corrective management authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfit candidate configs, invalid contracts, failing replay and incorrect task results are detectable; evidence quality and safety are safeguarded but not used to audit whole-current management reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: EvolveValidator checks canonicalization, contract, replay and policy and may reject a proposed evolved config. Benchmark evaluator/LLM judge may score task output; a rejection returns to next harness edit or run fallback, not to any legitimate S3 owner required to correct current organizational commitments. - Decision owner: Validation and replay implementation plus externally configured benchmark grader; no separate organizational audit with independent direct operating-fact access and governing S3 corrective return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validation report, changeset, replay smoke check, novelty/evidence gate, cross-round journal and per-task scoring. - Closure path: Proposed config → validity/replay/policy check → permit or reject for next experiment. Missing independent observation of S3 current-performance claim → challenge → binding S3 remedy applied across S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate technical validation gate can be effective without being S3: source shows config/test validity and task scoring, not a complementary management reality-check function with its own return. - Evidence: harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/replay.py. - Basis: First-party strongest audit-candidate source path inspected and bounded to config/task checking. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External independent benchmarks are not automatically first-party independent organizational auditor; their scoring policy cannot be borrowed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Use encountered external task/evaluation outcomes to autonomously redesign future agent-harness capabilities, validate new viable options and commit an accepted changed configuration for later operating rounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Past harness may fail unknown future tasks; patterns in new environments and actual trajectories reveal deficiencies in instructions, processors, tools, and resource/safety boundaries that motivate future changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed MetaAgent diagnoses prior task trajectories and autonomously writes candidate changed HarnessConfig plus prompt/processor implementations. EvolveValidator enforces source-owned validity/contract/replay/novelty gates; the shipped multi-round recipe promotes a valid candidate into durable round config and sets it as currentconfig for the next evaluator/task-agent execution. - Decision owner: Configured MetaAgent model for proposed future capability and technical validation controller for admission; the first-party executable evolver recipe is the accepted-change authority within the explicitly bounded experiment. No claim of wider business approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trajectory collection and benchmark feedback, journal of hypotheses/round history, model-backed config editor, write scope and budget, canonicalization/contract/replay checks, output config plus promoted round state/resume file. - Closure path: External evaluation tasks and failures → task trajectories and cross-round context → MetaAgent model diagnoses/generates alternative future prompt/tools/processors → EvolveValidator checks new config → recipe promoteroundoutput and updates persisted lastoutputconfig / currentconfig → next round uses changed executable HarnessConfig and creates fresh feedback. - Boundary reachability: recipe/tb2evolver/run.py is a first-party executable: MetaAgent(...) constructed at line 635, each iteration reads current trajectories then calls metaagent.evolve at line 710, adapter promoteroundoutput at 718, state persists lastoutputconfig at 746, and currentconfig=promotedconfig at 751. Separate GAIA recipe writes currentconfig=candidatecfg after validation; no optional custom external adopter glue is needed. - External distinction: Task benchmark/environment outcomes are observed from separately executed tasks and actual candidate trajectories, not inferred from static configuration alone. They include failures and task variation across rounds; benchmark grader policy and provider internal operations stay outside HarnessX ownership. - Future / prospective distinction: The MetaAgent modifies reusable prompt/processor/tool harness configuration for following, distinct evaluation tasks and rounds, rather than merely choosing the best answer to the present user&#39;s one request. Round state and journal deliberately carry changed capability forward. - Adaptation option generated: A model-authorized configuration editor proposes new prompt templates, hook processors, configured tool paths and other allowed harness levers from observed failure patterns. Novelty and replay checks distinguish viable alternatives from a repeated/no-op or invalid modification. - Path back into current capability / S3: After successful source-owned postflight, the recipe promotes the new config path, records it in persisted run state and loads that same config as currentconfig for subsequent ordinary Harness.run task evaluation. This closes directly into the next S1 operating capability at the harness-improvement recursion; no independent organization-wide S3 is assumed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model selection of what harness should become, based on external task evidence, combines with first-party validation and actual promotion into later execution. This is a concrete prospective, executable capability loop and not only a memory/summary or evaluation score. - Evidence: harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/journal.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py; recipe/gaiaevolver/run.py; recipe/gaiaevolver/run.py. - Basis: Independent first-party S4 option/change/admission and installed next-round loop; positive limited to configured, source-wired harness-improvement experiment. - Confidence: Medium-high for source reachability and capability adoption, not benchmark effectiveness. - Caveats: The validator can reject proposed configs and produce no new capability; not every iteration improves scores. Promotion applies to experimental harness config rather than a managed external production service or approved enterprise deployment. User supplies experiment scope and models. First-party recipe may deliberately accept a valid but not empirically improved config; S4=A here indicates a future-capability adaptation mechanism, not guaranteed selection of a better policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No system-owned ultimate legitimate purpose/identity policy arbiter adjudicates what goals the harness-improvement organization must pursue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Allowed tool/build paths, model cost, replay bounds and evidence rules constrain experiments but do not legitimate a changed institutional purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human selects benchmark/task pool/provider, allowed write roots and budgets; EvolveValidator and gates permit/reject one future config according to those preset criteria. No S5 authority changes the ultimate ethical/purpose contract across the system. - Decision owner: External adopter/research experiment operator for goals, data rights and provider choices; first-party validation engines for local code safety, not final governing mission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Meta-agent write scope, run cost/timeout, canonical config schema, validation/novelty policy, benchmark setup and repo maintenance/license. - Closure path: Preset experiment objective → candidate design/replay → future config. Missing conflicting ultimate values/identity interpretations → legitimate final authority decision → enforceable mission/purpose return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source technical acceptance of one capability modification (S4) is narrower than authorizing the whole organization&#39;s ultimate identity and purpose (S5). - Evidence: harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/agent.py; harnessx/metaharness/validateworkflow.py; harnessx/metaharness/processors/writescopegate.py; recipe/tb2evolver/run.py. - Basis: First-party config-policy, safety and external institutional-right boundary inspected. - Confidence: Medium-high at declared recursion. - Caveats: The external university/company/deployer may own legal purpose; this package does not instantiate such a VSM owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4143efce3ef5f626dc3211866f7cd1409773ec6d/assessments/harnessx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4143efce3ef5f626dc3211866f7cd1409773ec6d\">4143efc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T22:25:19Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Growth-Circle/cadis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">C.A.D.I.S.</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Growth-Circle/cadis/tree/52c55854b1abbcda82839b7a934cbdb16a69b635\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 52c55854b1abbcda82839b7a934cbdb16a69b635\">52c5585 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user objectives through a model-driven tool/action loop that receives environment/tool observations and chooses subsequent operational action until completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request ambiguity, workspace/file/git/shell state, tool results, provider responses, failed actions and intermediate evidence that changes the next useful operational step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which available tool/action to invoke, its arguments, whether further action is needed and the final operational response within configured safety/step bounds. - Decision owner: the model-driven CADIS agent running through the daemon&#39;s first-party generation/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, parsetoolcalldirectives, native tool registry, workspace grants, policy engine, approvals, session/step limits, persistence and daemon event streaming. - Closure path: request enters cadisd → routed agent/model generates a tool directive or response → daemon executes the selected tool under policy/workspace controls → tool results are placed into a follow-up prompt → model chooses the next action or completes → result returns to the caller/environment. - Boundary reachability: ordinary message.send through the shipped daemon executes this first-party loop; no downstream organizational actor must be supplied to obtain model/action/feedback closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy, grants and bounds can reject or constrain choices, but they do not predetermine the substantive tool/action sequence. Removing the model actor removes the ordinary operational decision path. - Evidence: crates/cadis-daemon/src/main.rs; crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: direct client commands and deterministic policy/approval branches also exist. A is based on the standard model-owned tool/action path, not on every request transition being autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among distinct concurrently active CADIS operational agents/workers by isolating project mutations and bounding shared worker concurrency. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel coding units can corrupt or overwrite the same working tree, while unbounded fan-out can consume shared daemon capacity and delay peer work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise the cross-S1 isolation and shared-concurrency regime that determines where a coding worker may mutate files and whether another worker may begin now or must wait. - Decision owner: no autonomous first-party S2 actor owns that regime. The standard daemon supplies the function-specific worktree/queue relation and deterministically enforces parent/configured limits, leaving revision of the regime outside autonomous S2 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-worker worktree intent and metadata, Git worktree creation, workspace/grant checks, worktree-local worker command cwd, maxconcurrentworkers, global running-worker count, queued worker state and slot-release/start logic. - Closure path: multiple S1 tasks enter parallel worker execution → daemon allocates project work into separate owned worktrees and counts current running workers → excess workers remain Queued and isolated workers operate on separate branches/paths → freed slots start waiting workers and their subsequent execution proceeds under the isolated context. - Boundary reachability: worktree-backed worker operation and global worker admission are shipped daemon/core paths at the pinned revision; callers do not need to implement their own isolation or worker queue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the disturbance and attenuation relation are first-party and specific to cross-unit interference, but isolation/admission outcomes are mechanically derived from configured policy rather than selected by an autonomous coordinating model. This supports C, not A. - Evidence: crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/10RISKREGISTER.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic @agent routing and child-agent spawning are not credited as S2 by themselves. Positive credit rests on the documented parallel-edit/resource disturbances and the first-party isolation/backpressure relation. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately routed/spawned model-driven agents/agent sessions executing operational tasks inside the same cadisd, including coding tasks represented by daemon-managed worker records. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous project mutations can conflict on the same repository state, and concurrent worker fan-out can exceed shared worker execution capacity even when each task is locally valid. - Attenuating coordination relation: CADIS assigns project workers separate Git worktrees and applies a daemon-wide maximum number of running workers, holding excess work in Queued state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the owned worktree becomes the worker&#39;s execution context/cwd for validation and artifacts, while queued workers do not begin until a shared slot is released; both relations change where or when each S1&#39;s next operation occurs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited mechanisms are explicitly aimed at cross-agent file-conflict and aggregate fan-out disturbances, not merely at transporting messages, naming specialists or ordering a task graph.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a current organization-wide view of agent commitments and use it to allocate/change present operational work, with an additional legitimate parent/operator intervention mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicated or poorly allocated agent work, changing agent/session status, current task commitments, runaway/unwanted current activity and aggregate worker pressure can make the daemon&#39;s present operation incoherent or exceed current capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — decide from current all-agent state whether to create a new child agent and assign it a task; parent mode — inspect the current roster and terminate an agent or cancel a session, removing associated current commitments/workers from later operation. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — the main CADIS model actor receiving first-party current runtime state; parent mode — the legitimate self-hosting operator using shipped CLI/HUD/protocol controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestratorawarenesscontext(), agent/session registries and sorted snapshots, recent-session task/step summaries, bounded model-driven [SPAWN] parsing, spawn limits, global worker scheduler, agent.list, agent.kill, session.cancel, persistence and cancellation propagation. - Closure path: current agent roster/session state is assembled → injected into the main agent&#39;s prompt → main model selects a new child role/task when current organization needs it → runtime creates the child and task-bearing session → subsequent organization state includes the changed commitment. In parent mode, roster inspection → kill/cancel request → agent/session and non-terminal associated worker cancellation → subsequent live operation reflects the intervention. - Boundary reachability: whole-agent awareness and model-driven child spawning are standard first-party runtime paths; parent list/kill/cancel operations are stable shipped protocol/CLI/HUD surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main actor receives whole-organization current state rather than only one delegated task and autonomously changes current commitments. Deterministic spawn/worker limits constrain that authority but do not choose the child task. The parent path is separately owned by the operator, producing the (P) modifier. - Evidence: crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/29PROTOCOLFREEZE.md; crates/cadis-cli/src/main.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: ordinary delegation is not credited as S3 by itself. The positive autonomous mapping depends specifically on the main agent&#39;s injected whole-roster/recent-session state plus its live model-owned commitment-creation path. Worker concurrency limits are supporting current-control enforcement, not the reason for A. - Whole-system current view: orchestratorawarenesscontext() enumerates the current agent roster with status, specialist and latest task and also includes recent agent sessions with state and step/budget progress; this generated state is injected into the main orchestrator prompt. - Current-control decision scope: autonomous creation/allocation of new child-agent commitments in response to current organization state, plus parent termination/cancellation of existing live commitments; daemon worker admission additionally constrains concurrent execution.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit path is established for the declared CADIS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional reviewer/tester specialist roles, worker validation commands/test-report artifacts, patch-apply preflight, policy checks, approvals, logs and lifecycle events. - Closure path: not established as S3. These paths validate ordinary outputs or expose ordinary evidence but do not establish a materially independent complementary challenge of an S1/S3 operational claim with a required corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a role named Reviewer can be spawned/routed, but it uses the common agent contract and is not packaged as a mandatory complementary audit owner with distinct evidence access/authority. Deterministic validation/preflight is routine production QA/integrity control. - Evidence: docs/standards/10AGENTSTANDARD.md; crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/29PROTOCOLFREEZE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: a downstream organization can deliberately instantiate a reviewer with different workspace/test access. That would be a separate system-in-focus and does not upgrade the standard CADIS distribution here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established for the declared CADIS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as prospective adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent memory records/capsules, workspace/project facts, correction/procedure memory kinds, provider/model selection, specialist personas, configuration reload and project/workspace state. - Closure path: no first-party operating path was found that distinguishes an external/future capability issue, generates an adaptation option, selects it and returns a changed capability or S3 regime into subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cadis-memory can store candidate records, explicitly promote/reject them and inject already-confirmed capsules, but cadis-core only consumes confirmed memory. The standard runtime does not wire autonomous evidence-to-adaptation proposal/selection/promotion closure. - Evidence: crates/cadis-memory/src/lib.rs; crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established for the declared CADIS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at identity/ultimate-policy level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no legitimate first-party ultimate authority is wired to adjudicate or amend the organization&#39;s governing identity/policy when such an issue arises. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hardcoded/default CADIS identity prompt, specialist persona fields and agent.specialist.set, central policy engine, workspace grants, risk classes, approval state and ordinary model/provider configuration. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → adjudication/amendment → subsequent-operation-under-new-policy chain is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the named CADIS persona and parent-editable specialist persona configure operating roles; policy/approval components enforce already-selected constraints. Neither surface establishes ultimate-policy ownership merely because it changes prompts or blocks risky actions. - Evidence: crates/cadis-core/src/lib.rs; docs/29PROTOCOLFREEZE.md; crates/cadis-policy/src/lib.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence - Confidence: high - Caveats: agent.specialist.set and parent configuration can materially change behavior, but task/role/persona configuration is not automatically the organization&#39;s identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/76cc1d6c195ab75cb4dea51f540dd3d685cf4700/assessments/cadis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 76cc1d6c195ab75cb4dea51f540dd3d685cf4700\">76cc1d6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-07T15:23:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hikaMaeng/ndx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ndx</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-07\">2026-10-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hikaMaeng/ndx/tree/b1cb16e9b3b3fcd514c9b71a199e10490b7900d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1cb16e9b3b3fcd514c9b71a199e10490b7900d7\">b1cb16e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work by interpreting a user request, selecting first-party tools, inspecting/modifying the workspace, running commands or sandboxed tools, and iterating from tool results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, codebase state, tool/process results, errors, provider responses and evolving user/session context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next operational tool/action, interpret returned evidence, revise the approach, optionally delegate bounded work, and decide when the answer/work is complete. - Decision owner: the ndx root model-backed coding agent; child runAgent loops can own bounded delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent loop, tool registry/worker processes, Docker sandbox for eligible paths, session/runtime persistence, streaming events, max-turn guard, project/user context loading. - Closure path: user input → model decision → first-party tool action → tool result/history → next model decision → further action or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: runAgent is directly wired into the product runtime/session server and creates the first-party tool registry at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime executes, persists and constrains operations, but the task-specific choice of tool, interpretation of results and next action comes from the model loop. - Evidence: loop.ts, tool-execution.ts, architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external; sandbox/configuration and human environment bound reachable actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents can share one cwd and therefore can in principle interfere through overlapping files/dependencies, but the reviewed ndx path does not establish a concrete first-party attenuation policy for that disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decision right is established beyond generic delegation, messaging/waiting and per-child lifecycle operations. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent, sendinput/sendmessage, waitagent, per-record state, CSV worker creation, generic tool-result feedback. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can create well-scoped children and wait/message them, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat delegation, messaging, shared state or sequencing alone as S2. The TypeScript controller supplies no disjoint worktree/file-ownership assignment, collision detector, dependency frontier, mutual-exclusion policy or repair/feedback loop tied to inter-S1 interference. - Evidence: agents.ts, subagents.ts, agent-loop-analysis.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: spawnagentsoncsv declares concurrency-related arguments at the tool-schema layer, but the reviewed TypeScript implementation iterates rows and calls spawnAgent without implementing an S2-specific interference controller.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: observe and regulate the live set of subordinate child-agent commitments during the current coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: running/completed/failed/closed child work, children that should be waited on or terminated, the need to launch additional bounded work, and settled children that need a subsequent turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect child lifecycle snapshots, decide to spawn additional children, wait on selected children, close/abort a selected active child, and start a subsequent turn for a settled child. - Decision owner: the root ndx model-backed agent through model-callable collaboration tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared SubAgentController, stable child ids, per-agent status/error/finalText timestamps, AbortController, child promises, listagents, waitagent, closeagent, resume/follow-up/input tools. - Closure path: root requests current agent-set/status evidence → model decides a current intervention → controller applies spawn/wait/close/resume/follow-up action → updated lifecycle/result evidence returns as a tool result → root decides subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the collaboration tools are registered in the first-party tool registry and dispatch through ToolContext.agentController; both root runtime and nested child runs receive the shared controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the TypeScript controller stores status and executes lifecycle operations, while the model chooses which child to observe, wait on, stop, resume or task next. Removing the model leaves lifecycle machinery without the same task-specific current-control judgment. - Whole-system current view: listagents returns snapshots for the controller&#39;s known agent set, including status, task prompt, final text/error and timestamps; waitagent returns selected current/final snapshots after a bounded wait. - Current-control decision scope: child spawn, selective wait, targeted close/abort, resume/reuse of a closed child record, and follow-up/input into a non-running child. The reviewed controller does not provide Rust Codex&#39;s full mailbox/watch graph, so the S3 claim is bounded to this implemented child set. - Evidence: subagents.ts, agents.ts, agent-loop-analysis.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: sendinput on an already-running child returns a queued status in the reviewed implementation without a Rust-style durable mailbox; S3 is therefore credited from the implemented view/wait/close/spawn/reuse control surface, not from unsupported live-message semantics.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent first-party audit/reviewer loop with corrective closure is established inside the shipped ndx runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S3 independence/closure. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic child-agent creation, model-pool field named reviewer, repository-development .codex/skills/code-review, builtin command metadata named review, ordinary tests/tool results. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming/configuration surfaces do not instantiate a distinct reviewer owner. The reviewed TypeScript subagent path records agenttype metadata but launches the same runAgent config/client/tool environment; no first-party reviewer restriction, fresh evidence contract or returned veto/corrective gate is wired. - Evidence: subagents.ts, config/index.ts, session command registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository-maintainer code-review skills may help contributors review ndx itself, but they are excluded from the installed coding-agent organizational boundary and cannot donate S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective capability-adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md/skill discovery, provider/model settings, session history/logs, environment context and manual configuration. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ndx can load instructions/skills and users can alter configuration, but no reviewed first-party model loop autonomously identifies future/environmental change, generates an adaptation option and writes/promotes it into continuing product capability. - Evidence: initial-context/skill architecture described in agent-loop-analysis.md, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reloadable project/user resources are operator-authored extensibility, not autonomous S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured instructions, permission/sandbox settings, project/user AGENTS.md, tool/MCP configuration and local user authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these mechanisms constrain or configure current operation; they do not grant a model-backed actor legitimate authority to decide ndx&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose/policy and return that decision as binding governance. - Evidence: config/index.ts, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user/developer configuration is an external authority boundary, not by itself a parent-governed S5 loop under Methodology 0.3.6.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b9fcf4c55f11bb9f24139040fce58f2bd1bea53/assessments/ndx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b9fcf4c55f11bb9f24139040fce58f2bd1bea53\">9b9fcf4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T17:51:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rizwan3d/StemCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StemCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rizwan3d/StemCode/tree/8e52bb02310b91337bf6eb88e163fc65acb059f8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8e52bb02310b91337bf6eb88e163fc65acb059f8\">8e52bb0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing software-engineering work by interpreting the current objective, selecting permitted inspection/edit/shell/validation actions, executing them and revising subsequent actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository state, implementation alternatives, provider uncertainty, build/test/tool failures, incomplete plans, malformed/empty provider outputs and task-specific evidence discovered during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific model/tool action and revise that choice after observing tool/provider/workspace evidence. - Decision owner: the active model-backed StemCode primary actor; delegated child actors own the same local S1 discretion for their bounded task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/execution pipeline, active profile, permission overlays, session persistence, provider adapters, plan/loop guards, workspace instructions/memory, tracked file-edit transactions and interrupt/recovery handling. - Closure path: task/current session state → provider/model decision → first-party tool execution → result persisted and returned to the conversation → same actor selects another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the first-party AgentConversationPipeline and built-in profiles are the standard runtime used by the documented CLI/Desktop/editor/headless product surfaces; no adjacent development actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining tools, permissions, sessions and deterministic guards removes the task-specific choice of what to inspect, edit, run or validate next; those mechanisms constrain or transport rather than replace that discretion. - Evidence: AgentConversationPipeline.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: particular tools may require operator approval or be denied by profile/policy, but supported operation retains autonomous task-level decision/action loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among subordinate S1 units when a primary StemCode actor coordinates several bounded child tasks against the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous editing-capable subagents could overwrite, revert or otherwise conflict with one another in the shared repository; unconstrained delegated scopes can also create cross-task churn. - Distinct S1 units: separate child ReplSessionContext executions created by agentorchestrate, each running the first-party conversation pipeline under general or explore and returning its own tool/output/edit evidence. - Inter-S1 disturbance: editing-capable child actors share the parent workspace and can therefore contend on repository mutations; StemCode&#39;s own orchestration contract distinguishes these from read-only tasks and explicitly warns coordinated agents not to revert others&#39; changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary model chooses the child task set, subagent profiles, execution strategy and optional write scopes; auto then parallelizes consecutive read-only tasks but executes editing-capable tasks in controlled sequence, while parallelreadonly rejects mutating subagents entirely. Delegated prompts transmit task/write-scope/coordination constraints to each S1. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: strategy and write-scope choices alter when child S1s run and what workspace region they are instructed to touch; each child handoff/tool/edit result returns to the parent actor, which can integrate the evidence into subsequent operation or another coordinated handoff. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is explicitly conditioned on the concrete interference class of shared-workspace mutations. Read-only work is allowed to overlap; editing-capable work is deliberately serialized or rejected from the parallel mode, and bounded write scopes are passed to mutating children. This is targeted disturbance attenuation, not sequencing merely because tasks exist. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which bounded child activities should coexist, which agent profile should perform each, what coordination strategy to use and what write boundaries to assign before those S1s operate. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed StemCode actor owns the task-specific coordination discretion; deterministic orchestration code enforces the nonparallel mutation safety rule and concurrency limit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentOrchestrateTool; child-session construction; MaxParallelReadOnlyTasks; editing-capable classification; strategy validation; semaphore; sequential execution of mutating requests; delegated coordination/write-scope prompt; returned aggregate result. - Closure path: primary actor identifies independent/dependent side work → calls agentorchestrate with tasks/strategy/scopes → runtime schedules read-only parallelism and mutation serialization → child S1s receive coordination constraints and operate → their results/edit evidence return to the primary actor → later primary/child behaviour can be revised. - Boundary reachability: agentorchestrate is in the standard build profile and in supported inspection-oriented primary profiles where applicable; child general/explore profiles and the execution policy are shipped first-party runtime behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the primary actor is removed, deterministic code can still serialize a pre-existing request, but no task-specific actor remains to decide the coordinated task set, child profiles, strategy or write scopes. The runtime owns enforcement; the primary agent owns the bounded coordination choice. - Evidence: AgentOrchestrateTool.cs; AgentDelegationSupport.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this S2 is bounded to one orchestration handoff and the shared-workspace interference it regulates; it does not imply persistent organization-wide current control.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S3 whole-current control loop was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task decomposition, active-plan tracking, permission enforcement, orchestration batch scheduling and token/budget limits exist, but no distinct whole-system current-operation variety is shown being regulated through S3 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live plan/update-plan machinery, bounded subagent orchestration, permission profiles, budget controls, session state, interrupts and deterministic concurrency limits. - Closure path: absent at S3 level; no first-party persistent whole-system current view → shared-resource/commitment/priority intervention → returned change to an independently evolving operational population was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the primary model does allocate bounded delegated work, but that is parent-task decomposition inside one awaited orchestration call. The reviewed path does not expose the whole-system current view and current-control authority required by the Profile. - Evidence: AgentOrchestrateTool.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; AgentConversationPipeline.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future/background-task machinery could change this conclusion if it supplied a population view plus substantive intervention rights; that path is not established in the frozen revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide an audit-specific, read-only review path over a proposed change set that is separate from the ordinary implementation actor and can challenge its claims with findings about bugs, regressions, unsafe assumptions, edge cases and missing tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that the normal implementation/reporting path may miss defects or weak tests in a PR/change set. - Claim being audited: that a proposed repository change is correct/safe enough with no material unreported regressions or testing gaps. - Ordinary reporting path: the ordinary coding actor edits/runs validation and reports completion through its primary session/tool loop; routine build/test results remain part of that production path. - Complementary access path: shipped GitHub review automation computes the independent base/head diff, invokes a separate read-only pr-reviewer profile against that artifact and publishes findings as a PR review/comment, with an uploaded review artifact. - Independence boundary: the reviewer is a separately invoked StemCode session/profile with read-only/safe-inspection permissions and receives the PR diff rather than inheriting the implementation actor&#39;s conversational claims. However the committed workflow&#39;s PR trigger is commented out and the path does not autonomously drive corrective execution back into the ordinary coding actor. - Who acts on findings: the hosting-platform/user/developer workflow consuming the posted PR findings; first-party StemCode does not in this frozen standard setup automatically convert those findings into a corrective implementation turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party tooling exposes the audit-specific independent judgment and outbound findings path, but the autonomous corrective authority/return closure must still be composed by the adopter. - Decision owner: constructor state. StemCode supplies the independent model-backed reviewer and findings publication primitive; deployment wiring and downstream corrective authority remain outside the closed autonomous product loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pr-reviewer read-only profile; base/head diff construction; review script; GitHub workflow permissions/artifact upload; PR comment/review publication. - Closure path: first-party path reaches PR findings publication but stops before autonomous corrective control; an adopter must compose the findings-consumer/repair return path for full closure. - Boundary reachability: the audit path is explicitly shipped and documented as StemCode CI review automation with first-party scripts/profile/workflow, so the S3-specific constructor is reachable without borrowing a third-party reviewer. The missing autonomous corrective return is why the state is C, not A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment itself is made by a separate model-backed reviewer, not by a deterministic parser. The incomplete part is organizational closure from findings back into corrective operation, so the assessment does not publish A. - Evidence: .github/stemcode-github-review.sh; .github/workflows/stemcode-review.yml; .stemcode/agents/pr-reviewer.md; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository co-location alone is not used as evidence. The positive state is intentionally only the shipped audit-specific constructor path; ordinary review profile use inside the same production conversation and routine tests are not credited as S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop over StemCode&#39;s own current capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web search, codebase intelligence, repository/team memory and optional lessons expose useful information for task execution and reuse, but they do not establish S4 at the assessed organizational recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: websearch, headless browser, codebase/LSP tools, repo memory, lesson memory, automatic tool-failure observation, skills and configurable profiles/providers. - Closure path: no external/future distinction → adaptation-option development → return into present organizational capability/S3 loop was established. Lesson retrieval returns prior local mistakes/fixes to future prompts but is not an external-and-prospective adaptation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can use web/repository evidence for the current task and can save/retrieve lessons, but that is current-task sensing/learning unless tied to future-oriented organizational adaptation of capability. - Evidence: BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; StemCode/Application/Tools/LessonMemoryTool.cs; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future support for agent-owned capability/profile/tool adaptation driven by environmental/future modeling could establish S4; current reusable memory alone does not.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, profiles, workspace instructions, budgets, approval prompts and operator configuration constrain ordinary operation, but no S5-level identity/policy issue is adjudicated through a runtime ultimate-authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5; developers/operators author configuration and ordinary approval choices outside any qualifying first-party S5 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in profile permission overlays; allow/deny/ask rules; workspace files/instructions; provider/model configuration; budget controls; operator prompts and runtime enforcement. - Closure path: absent at S5 level; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing subsequent operation path was found in the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model is constrained by configured policy but does not own the ultimate right to redefine StemCode&#39;s identity or top-level policy; static configuration and ordinary tool approval are specifically insufficient for S5. - Evidence: BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; README.md; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository maintainer governance is adjacent to the product boundary and is not imported as runtime S5 merely because it determines future releases.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/52c1740f0432c2d27847af8501de8f2772f55a86/assessments/st/stemcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 52c1740f0432c2d27847af8501de8f2772f55a86\">52c1740 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T04:23:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/get-vix/vix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/get-vix/vix/tree/e68a25c7df685335f5a202844988d492fe3c1b16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e68a25c7df685335f5a202844988d492fe3c1b16\">e68a25c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify software to satisfy a coding objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/test failures, context variation, provider responses and bounded delegated investigations/implementation work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive code/tool actions, interpret evidence and decide how to satisfy the engineering objective. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Vix agent or a model-backed workflow/subagent step owning its bounded operational outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon tool dispatch, sandbox/permission checks, workflow engine, session state, provider adapters, timeouts and budgets. - Closure path: objective → agent selects coding/tool action → runtime executes and returns evidence → agent revises work until completion/blocking. - Boundary reachability: the standard interactive agent, workflow agent steps and scheduled headless runs all invoke the shipped first-party model/tool runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic machinery carries/enforces actions; the model chooses the substantive engineering response. - Evidence: README.md; internal/config/defaults/agents/general.md; internal/daemon/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tools and workflows constrain the action surface without owning the coding judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material standard-distribution path is established that specifically attenuates an evidenced interference/oscillation between distinct operational S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance is placed under a first-party coordination loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent, background task IDs, taskoutput, workflow edges/forks, per-step budgets and separate threads transport/decompose work but do not themselves regulate a concrete peer-operational collision. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models can delegate or run parallel children, but delegation/parallelism alone does not establish coordination; no standard collision/oscillation response with feedback into both/affected S1 units was found. - Distinct S1 units: independent spawned agents or workflow/job agent steps can own bounded outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous write-capable units could conflict in shared project state, but the reviewed standard path does not document a corresponding S2-specific attenuation relation. - Attenuating coordination relation: none established beyond generic sequencing/routing/thread isolation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: no S2-specific collision result/negotiation path established. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not; the inspected primitives remain generic delegation/workflow transport for this purpose. - Evidence: internal/daemon/subagent.go; internal/daemon/toolschemas.go; internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a custom workflow can serialize work, but static workflow ordering is not sufficient S2 evidence under the active Profile.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous or constructor S3 current-control loop is established at the declared standard boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no whole-system current operational disturbance is placed under a first-party supervisory decision right. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow budgets/conditions, job scheduler state, timeout/auto-disable, task lists and user plan approval enforce or expose selected constraints but do not supply the required whole-system current-control discretion. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary agents own local task execution; deterministic workflow/scheduler machinery owns no discretionary whole-system S3 judgment, and the Plan approval gate occurs before execution rather than regulating a live operational whole. - Whole-system current view: job/thread/workflow status surfaces exist, but no standard autonomous owner is wired to use them as a whole-system current-control view. - Current-control decision scope: no qualifying standard owner/path established over current resource/commitment/priorities on behalf of the whole. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json; internal/daemon/jobs/scheduler.go; internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users can approve plans and manage jobs; those local/configuration interventions are not promoted to S3 without the Profile&#39;s whole-system current-control closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit an operational agent&#39;s completion claim against current project state and return gaps into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing requirements, unverifiable implementation or defects that ordinary worker self-report can miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make an independent pass/fail audit judgment from direct project evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed verify/reviewer agent running in a distinct workflow step/context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal workflow routing, structured JSON verdict, read/bash/code-intelligence tool access and deterministic pass/fail branch. - Closure path: pursue agent signals complete → separate verify agent independently inspects current state → verifier returns pass/fail plus feedback → fail routes feedback into a new pursue iteration; pass allows wrap-up. - Boundary reachability: Goal is a bundled standard workflow and its verifier/pursue routing is shipped in the default workflow configuration; reviewer agent/prompt is bundled first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workflow code routes the result, but the separate model-backed auditor makes the evidence-based judgment. - Claim being audited: the operational worker&#39;s claim that the requested goal is complete. - Ordinary reporting path: pursue&#39;s completion signal and ordinary implementation/tool results. - Complementary access path: a separate verifier/reviewer can read current repository state, run checks and form its own evidence-based verdict rather than trusting the worker transcript. - Independence boundary: distinct workflow agent step/reviewer role with its own run context and evidence-gathering tools; the Goal verify prompt is explicitly an independent audit. - Who acts on findings: deterministic workflow routing returns rejected-audit feedback to the operational pursue agent, which must address the gaps before signalling completion again. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json; internal/config/defaults/prompts/goal/verify.md; internal/config/defaults/agents/reviewer.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 is credited to the independent evidence path and corrective return, not merely to the name &quot;reviewer&quot; or to ordinary self-tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: construct recurring external/future sensing workflows whose model-backed reaction can develop and apply adaptation to the current project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future time-based needs and changing external conditions such as new pull requests, dependency/security changes, remote state or other polled events. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the composed scheduled agent step can interpret newly sensed external evidence and choose an adaptive project response; the standard distribution exposes the sensing, gating, model-run and unattended-write path, while the concrete adaptation objective/authority is supplied by the composed job/workflow. - Decision owner: constructor state — Vix supplies the function-specific first-party primitives and an autonomous general-agent runtime, but the developer/operator/creating agent must compose the concrete external distinction, prospective objective and return policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon cron/at scheduler, prompt resolution, bash polling, executeif gating, workflow agent steps, auto-write permissions, timeout/budget controls and persisted job state. - Closure path: composed external/time trigger → first-party polling/sensing step → conditional model-backed agent interprets the changed environment and develops a response → unattended workflow may write/act in project cwd → subsequent project capability/operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the jobs skill and scheduler are shipped first-party; jobs run through the daemon as isolated headless Vix threads and can invoke named/inline workflows using the standard general agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the eventual reacting agent can be autonomous, but the standard distribution does not pre-close one concrete external-and-prospective adaptation conversation; composition supplies the environmental model/objective/authority, so the published base state is C. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md; internal/daemon/jobs/scheduler.go; internal/daemon/jobrunner.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a reminder or event reaction alone is not S4; C rests on the first-party watcher/workflow construction path being specifically capable of sensing external/future distinctions, invoking an autonomous option-developing agent and returning action into project capability. - External distinction: the polling recipe explicitly senses changes outside the current coding run, such as newly opened pull requests or analogous remote/project-environment events. - Future / prospective distinction: cron/at triggers and persisted jobs defer sensing and model judgment to later environmental states rather than only reacting inside the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: the conditionally invoked general agent can interpret the new external evidence and develop a project response, such as a risk treatment or code/configuration change, under the composed workflow objective. - Path back into current capability / S3: the scheduled agent runs in the target project cwd with workflow/tool authority (including unattended writes when enabled), so a composed adaptation decision can change current project capability/operation; Vix supplies this return path while the concrete S4 loop remains constructor-composed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level conflict/proposal is routed through authoritative S5 closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sandbox, deny lists, workflow denytools, permission/confirmation surfaces, prompts and configuration constrain local operation without becoming identity authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents operate within developer/operator-authored policy and cannot authoritatively redefine Vix&#39;s identity or ultimate governing principles. - Evidence: README.md; internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md; internal/daemon/sandbox.go. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human confirmation and permissions are operational safety decisions, not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ee4c77759afcb8c999e46092bd300973e782fced/assessments/vix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ee4c77759afcb8c999e46092bd300973e782fced\">ee4c777 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/ClawGUI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ClawGUI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/ClawGUI/tree/d990d3e3f2390ced708771d9681d3c4f9a6cbef5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d990d3e3f2390ced708771d9681d3c4f9a6cbef5\">d990d3e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: execute user-requested GUI/device outcomes through autonomous perception, planning/tool orchestration and screen actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing UI state, device/application differences, task instructions, failed actions and environmental interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven agent actors choose operational plans/actions and adapt to observed device state. - Decision owner: autonomous brain/GUI-agent actor in the first-party deployment path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: device adapters, sessions, memory, chat surfaces and skill retrieval/injection. - Closure path: actions change device state, subsequent screen observations return to the agent and drive later actions until completion/failure. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - The two-agent brain/phone architecture separates responsibilities and tool execution, but the pinned evidence does not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation plus a mutual-adjustment loop that attenuates it. Orchestration/delegation alone is not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The brain orchestrates a task and the runtime manages sessions/device execution, but no distinct first-party actor is evidenced with current whole-organization visibility plus discretionary authority over shared resources, priorities or commitments across multiple operational S1s. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A. - Claim being audited: whether the operating PhoneAgent actually achieved the user&#39;s GUI task and, when not, where observable execution first departed from success. - Ordinary reporting path: executor rollout/actions and its normal task outcome. - Complementary access path: an explicitly independent verifier receives only sealed external evidence — screenshots, action log, accessibility/DOM data, final state and optional prior assertions — and never sees the executor&#39;s reasoning, plans or skill package. - Independence boundary: the verifier is instructed to judge only observable evidence and cannot modify the executor&#39;s playbook itself. - Decisive judgment / owner: the verifier model independently determines task success, failure type/step, root cause and behavioral corrections. - Supporting mechanisms: strict JSON schema, evidence packaging and restricted skill-file tools. - Closure path: failed verification feeds a separate skillrevise step that is required to change the skill package; when a revision is produced, PhoneAgent immediately retries using the changed capability. - Why this is S3: the path provides materially different access to operational reality, an independent judgment boundary and explicit corrective return rather than routine executor self-checking. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - The evolve loop changes future procedural capability, but its trigger and evidence are failures from the system&#39;s own current GUI-task execution. It does not establish an external-and-prospective model of changing users, threats, tools or future conditions that develops adaptation options in a two-way S3/S4 conversation. - Self-improvement alone is therefore not promoted to S4. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - User requests, model/configuration choices, skill modes and restricted edit surfaces constrain operation, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is evidenced at this recursion. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/clawgui.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:21:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dxiv/dxa-deimos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Deimos</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dxiv/dxa-deimos/tree/933a20c064294cb84dafaae2fe0dae617eb44c48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 933a20c064294cb84dafaae2fe0dae617eb44c48\">933a20c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue uncertain coding outcomes through iterative model-selected file, shell, search and other first-party tool actions, including individual teammate tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete coding instructions, new source state, command/tool outcomes, model/provider errors, changing team work and feedback from other agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next tool/operation or completion after observing the previous tool/agent result. - Decision owner: a configured local/cloud inference model operating in a first-party Deimos query/agent-tool loop; external API only supplies inference and transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK adapter and provider profiles, tool dispatcher, terminal session state, permission gates, context handling, subprocess/file tools and model selection. - Closure path: user or team assignment → model request → selected first-party action/tool → tool output → next model turn/operation or completion returned to user/team lead. - Boundary reachability: the public CLI launcher loads the built native Deimos entrypoint; the own-source query engine and AgentTool produce action/feedback cycles. Team spawning is reachable from the documented first-party optional mode and not from an adjacent hosted agent alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model while leaving API adapters and tools in place removes the context-sensitive decision about coding actions; static runtime mechanisms cannot replace the operational choice. - Evidence: bin/deimos.mjs; src/entrypoints/cli.tsx; src/query.ts; src/QueryEngine.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/AgentTool/runAgent.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is evidence for native decision-right mapping, not live benchmark accuracy or successful packaging of every optional tool.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate actual contention and premature succession between distinct active operational teammates through shared task-ownership conflict checks and dependency admission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent attempts by two teammates to claim one task, overcommitment of a busy agent, and a successor task begun before its dependencies finish. - Decisive decision or feedback right: file-lock-serialized, owner/dependency/busy-state checked admission or rejection of a claim; result controls whether a given S1 may start the task. - Decision owner: native deterministic first-party coordination constructor; teammate models may select subsequent eligible work but no separately evidenced autonomous agent owns the conflict-specific arbitration policy. Hence C, not automatic A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proper-lockfile, disk task list, owner/blockedBy graph, watcher, in-process runner, TaskUpdate and mailbox status. - Closure path: teammate sees an unclaimed task → tries claimTask → exclusive lock plus owner/blocked/busy checks → refusal or approved claim returned to watcher/runner → only accepted worker begins its operational prompt; blocked or contending agents wait or inspect newly available work. - Distinct S1 units: two named first-party team workers handling separate repository modifications under a single team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous ownership claims and incompatible active dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party atomic locking and task dependency checks, not merely a shared mailbox or generic queue. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: task-list watcher/in-process runner uses returned success/failure to select or defer its next prompt; failure to submit releases the claim. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implemented relation directly identifies and rejects a concrete conflict between independent operating agents before work proceeds, and delivers that arbitration into the operational selection loop. - Boundary reachability: native DEIMOSEXPERIMENTALAGENTTEAMS/flag opt-in enables TeamCreate and teammate spawn; the shipped useTaskListWatcher and inProcessRunner actually call the same src/utils/tasks.ts claimTask method. No external runtime is needed for the collision rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic lock arbitration remains even if agentic reasoning is removed; it establishes constructed S2 conflict regulation, not model-owned S2 policy. - Evidence: src/utils/tasks.ts; src/hooks/useTaskListWatcher.ts; src/utils/swarm/inProcessRunner.ts; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: direct manual TaskUpdate ownership editing is distinct from the protected claimTask code; the positive relation is the runtime automatic-claim path, not a universal no-conflicts guarantee.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain cohesion over an opted-in current team by assigning, revising and supervising several ongoing operational obligations and available teammates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked dependent work, idle or overburdened teammates, unexpected subtask failures, stale commitments and competing priorities in the active team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-directed team lead may choose team formation, current owner/priority allocation, updated dependencies, interventions to unblock work, instructions to teammates and shutdown/reallocation of the current team. - Decision owner: first-party team-lead model at the local team recursion in supported opt-in mode; task persistence and mailbox delivery merely enforce/communicate its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamCreate/team file/roster, TaskList/TaskUpdate, SendMessage, worker status and shared task metadata. - Whole-system current view: the team-lead can inspect the complete team task list with owner/status/blocker information and receive teammate completion/blocked or idle messages, rather than merely the contents of one operative tool call. - Current-control decision scope: priority/ownership among several currently operative teammates, resource allocation and intervention through task ownership and communication. - Closure path: teammate blocks/completes or signals difficulty → lead reads whole-team state/messages → model chooses reassignment/dependency adjustment/worker instruction → TaskUpdate/SendMessage records/delivers decision → subsequent teammate activity changes. - Boundary reachability: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts initializes native team lead and shared task list in the enabled first-party mode; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts and src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts expose ongoing obligations and discretionary owner/dependency changes; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts returns consequential directions to native teammates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic task store cannot decide what current team obligations should be changed in response to evolving progress; the model controls this whole-current choice rather than only forwarding messages. - Evidence: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts; src/tools/TeamCreateTool/prompt.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts; src/utils/swarm/teamHelpers.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: only the explicitly opt-in team mode supplies whole-current control; default single coder and user approvals are not re-labelled as S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no enabled independent complementary operational-evidence review with a consequential findings-return loop is established in the open Deimos distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source changes and task-completion claims may be wrong, but routine local reviews/tests and maintainer CI do not establish this additional organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor would inspect distinct primary artifacts, judge contradictory evidence and inform current control; the frozen open build instead disables the named specialized verifier and has no completed native alternative audit path. - Decision owner: no positive native S3 owner established. Internal verifier code exists as an adjacent disabled feature; the main coding agent controls ordinary local review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local /review prompt and /security-review, test/code tools, optional external /ultrareview, and maintainer CodeQL/CI—all outside a first-party independent complementary audit close. - Closure path: ordinary coder may read source or tests and continue S1; no alternative first-party auditor with separate judgment and a returned metasystemic control correction is operationally established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: verificationAgent presence is not sufficient when build-time feature(&#39;VERIFICATIONAGENT&#39;) is false and runtime GrowthBook gate defaults false, and a local model requested to review its own work is not an independent complementary audit by name. - Evidence: scripts/build.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/builtInAgents.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/verificationAgent.ts; src/tools/VerifyPlanExecutionTool/VerifyPlanExecutionTool.ts; src/tools/ReviewArtifactTool/ReviewArtifactTool.ts; src/commands/review.ts; src/commands/security-review.ts; .github/workflows/codeql.yml. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the frozen open-build boundary. - Caveats: this does not assert that developer CI, an internal build, or a separately hosted external service lacks review/audit. Their decision rights are not credited to this assessed publicly shipped CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed externally prospective adaptation mechanism that generates future capability options and returns them into current S3 oversight was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latency/cost changes among inference providers, new task and repository context and externally hosted service capabilities are addressed by operational routing/configuration rather than a prospective organizational intelligence cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured Python provider sidecar may select a current backend by observed health/latency; user/provider profiles govern model selection. This is not a future-directed adaptation judgment over the team&#39;s capabilities. - Decision owner: no evidenced first-party S4 at the local installed coding/agent-team recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: python/smartrouter.py experimental sidecar, provider bootstrap/discovery, model choice and task planning; PROACTIVE=false and AGENTTRIGGERS=false in scripts/build.ts. - Closure path: current model/backend switch informs the next inference request, not a future-state/options decision returned into S3 control to transform longer-run capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider health scoring and context-dependent code plan are operational, not environmental foresight; future-looking labels on disabled features cannot supply an implemented S4 loop. - Evidence: python/smartrouter.py; scripts/provider-recommend.ts; scripts/build.ts; src/commands/ultraplan.tsx; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately managed user organization may assess future models and alter its installation, but that external governance is not native S4 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-level identity/ultimate policy decision path is established for this assessed local agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, model/provider choice, tool permissions, usage limits and opt-in team access are operational constraints rather than conflicts about organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/developers choose settings and approve particular tools, while the runtime enforces fixed rules; no identity/policy choice closing S3–S4 tension is observed. - Decision owner: no native ultimate-policy owner; external human or maintainer may govern their broader project independently. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config/profile editor, local permission gates, policy-limit helpers, source build flags and team mode enablement. - Closure path: a local tool action is approved/rejected or a mode toggled and coding continues; no higher-order identity issue → legitimate policy authority → binding returned system-operation constraint is wired as a first-party S5 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static source-build policy and local operator approvals are not an autonomous S5 decision or a legitimate parent-mode ultimate-policy return. - Evidence: src/services/policyLimits/index.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; scripts/build.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; README.md; LEGAL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organizational/legal governance recorded in public docs is adjacent, not automatically an operating parent mode of the Deimos CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bd599e834eea44b568ccea9819d5e49adbf7061f/assessments/deimos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bd599e834eea44b568ccea9819d5e49adbf7061f\">bd599e8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:25:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/525300887039/BareAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BareAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/525300887039/BareAgent/tree/1dfd1057d372e872f54c0a666f375dce2bc11a26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1dfd1057d372e872f54c0a666f375dce2bc11a26\">1dfd105 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - Function: Autonomously perform uncertain developer coding tasks through file/shell/tool actions and observed feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository state, errors, test failures, unavailable resources, and follow-up developer questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root model or each teammate model chooses which action to execute and how to respond to the returned result. - State: A - Decision owner: First-party model-driven root/teammate agent loops; the provider supplies inference while the built-in runtime carries out local actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed tool schemas/handlers, hooks/permissions, retry policy, message persistence/compaction, shell and file tools, background execution. - Closure path: Work request → model-selected action → tool/handler result → model feedback in the same coding loop → next action or final result; background teammate handles a message or claimed task using the same loop. - Boundary reachability: Default REPL invokes native agentloop; the documented /team spawn path creates independent running AutonomousAgent workers that invoke agentloop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool execution and permissions do not pick a context-appropriate coding action without the operative model. - Evidence: core/loop.py; main.py; autonomous.py; tools.py. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - Function: Attenuate duplicate execution of one shared pending task when several autonomous teammate S1 workers see the same ready item. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running teammates can concurrently select the same pending task before either finishes; duplicate claims/work would waste work or conflict on the same task commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The shared task manager compares a task&#39;s expected status against current status and atomically commits the first claim; a rejected claimant re-evaluates the ready set rather than executing that task. - State: C - Decision owner: First-party deterministic claim-and-retry constraint; a developer/agent must still supply any discretionary policy for arbitration or rescheduling beyond first-winner conflict attenuation. No S2 coordinating agent owns adaptive arbitration as shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager RLock, expectedstatus compare-and-update, persisted .tasks.json, dependency readiness, per-teammate run loops. - Closure path: Concurrent ready-task observation → first atomic pending→inprogress update → competing update raises ValueError → second teammate skips the lost claim and searches other ready tasks instead. - Boundary reachability: TaskManager instance is shared by the main REPL and spawned AutonomousAgent workers through maketeamhandlers; workers invoke claimtask for ready work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision-sensitive response is embedded in explicit first-party code but chosen by lock/CAS rules, not by a model&#39;s independent coordination judgment, so it supports C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: Separately spawned AutonomousAgent background threads, each with its own provider, prompt, handler set, and task execution loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing acquisition of the same ready persisted task by two running teammate agents. - Attenuating coordination relation: Compare-and-set claim on shared task status and losing-claim rejection within the worker claim loop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Losing worker does not execute a task it failed to claim; it resumes scan or waits, while winning worker marks done/failed on completion. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This path rejects a concrete competing claim by two operating workers and changes which worker may proceed; the generic message bus, team routing and static workflow DAG are not used as independent S2 evidence. - Evidence: tasks.py update/getreadytasks; AutonomousAgent claim loop; shared TaskManager wiring. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - Function: Own whole-current commitments and interventions for a live team of independently executing coding S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple running teammate commitments, pending/done/failed tasks and developer requests can require reassignment, roster adjustment, work reprioritization, shutdown, or additional work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In agent mode the root model may inspect task and team rosters, create/update tasks, register/spawn/shutdown workers, and send targeted work requests based on responses. In separate parent mode the human does equivalent current intervention via /team and task command surfaces. - State: A(P) - Decision owner: Autonomous root model in first-party agent-tool mode; human operator for the separately exposed command-controlled parent mode. Background manager and TaskManager transport/enforce these decisions but do not autonomously choose current priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamlist, teamregister, teamspawn, teamsend, teamshutdown; tasklist/taskcreate/taskupdate; per-session MessageBus and background worker state; running-thread probe; reply-to-request protocol. - Closure path: Root agent sees whole-team roster/task status and teammate results → chooses new current allocation or intervention → concrete team/task tool action → updated task commitments, active teammate population, or returned worker instruction → subsequent worker execution/feedback. - Boundary reachability: Team and task tool schemas are standard first-party tools registered through core/tools.py and connected by buildhandlers/maketeamhandlers in main.py. /team commands address the same handlers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The root model can decide when to change team composition, who handles a request, or which commitments to create. A deterministic scheduler only enforces claims; a human alternative exists without removing autonomous tool-mode choice. - Whole-system current view: teamlist returns all registered workers with actual running status; tasklist returns current work records and statuses; teamsend and mailbox responses provide subordinate results. - Current-control decision scope: Create/change a task commitment, launch/stop a worker, and send/revise an operational request across a whole current team; this is not solely selecting a single child function call. - Evidence: TEAMTOOLSCHEMAS and task tools; team handlers and /team commands; worker task behavior; task ledger. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium: meaningful current-control reachability is clear; a live multi-worker strategy adaptation trace has not been independently reproduced - Caveats: Default solo mode is only one S1 and does not exercise a team-level S3. Positive S3 is credited to the documented first-party team mode, not the bare presence of a queue or generic model planning.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - Function: No operationally guaranteed complementary independent audit of claims about teammate execution with a return to whole-current control was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A teammate may produce faulty work or a weak plan; ordinary teamrequestreview solicits a model&#39;s opinion on the plan, not a verified independent observation of what the worker actually did. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root model or human requests a review; a nominated teammate writes an answer. The standard PLANAPPROVAL path does not require sampled operational artifacts or separate protected evidence before an audit verdict is returned. - State: — - Decision owner: Requested reviewer model for plan opinion and the requester for next action; no distinct source-first operational audit authority guaranteed by shipped contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProtocolFSM PLANAPPROVAL request/response, separate provider-backed teammate, shared read-capable tools, per-request prompt wrapper, optional tests and traces. - Closure path: Requester-supplied plan → teammate review prompt → textual verdict → requester. Missing guaranteed independent operational evidence → audit judgment → whole-current management correction circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent worker identity alone is not sufficient for S3; normal plan approval does not establish sufficiently complementary access to operational reality. - Evidence: teamrequestreview; buildincomingprompt; plan approval protocol; team docs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - Function: No first-party external-and-prospective option-modeling function with feedback into whole-current capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project knowledge, user demands or reusable workflows may affect future work, but first-party skill reflection captures previous local actions and user-promoted drafts rather than prospectively modeling external change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reflection model may propose a reusable skill; the human decides whether to promote; neither step demonstrates a distinct outside/future intelligence decision coupled to S3 current control. - State: — - Decision owner: Current coding model for task learning/drafts, operator for promotion; no attributable S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SkillGenerator two-threshold trigger, isolated reflection model run, pending skill store, /skill keep promotion, model/provider configuration. - Closure path: Accumulated local work → LLM reflects and drafts skill → human promotes into later skill catalog. Missing independent externally sensed change, prospective option appraisal, and return into current team strategy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic experiential learning or reusable procedures do not satisfy the Profile&#39;s external-and-future condition by themselves. - Evidence: skillgen.py; runskillreflection and promotion; skill catalog. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - Function: No first-party ultimate organizational identity/constitutional policy arbitration loop is evidenced at the live coding team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission limits, developer config, workspace rules and team role descriptions constrain actions but do not resolve ultimate purpose/policy conflicts for the team organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human config sets security permissions/roles; root agent may define teammate work roles. Neither is a proven ultimate identity-policy authority with return to the operating whole. - State: — - Decision owner: Human for local permissions and safety preferences; deterministic enforcement for tool operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionGuard, team role/system-prompt registry, sandbox, approval hooks, config and fail-closed child permissions. - Closure path: User sets local constraint → permission or role handler applies it to a tool/worker. No S3–S4 identity conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy decision → whole-team return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task permissions, system prompts and teammate role selection remain execution governance, not proof of constitutive identity decisions. - Evidence: permissions; teammate roles; team composition; hooks. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f07cb3f6ee281940aa69e93dd7a06b494ec31be8/assessments/bareagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f07cb3f6ee281940aa69e93dd7a06b494ec31be8\">f07cb3f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T09:09:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/haorui-harry/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/haorui-harry/agent-harness/tree/109dde248cb44227a9ed3a204790432a6da843da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 109dde248cb44227a9ed3a204790432a6da843da\">109dde2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an open-ended user request into concrete workspace/tool/evidence actions and a delivered artifact through a persistent thread execution whose operational plan can be model-selected and revised from execution feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, available capabilities, current workspace state, tool/evidence results, failed or missing artifacts/validation, and changing completion gaps during the run. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in live-enabled mode, choose the substantive task-graph actions to perform and revise those actions after observing current execution state and completion gaps. - Decision owner: the configured live model actor reached through first-party task-profile expansion/subagent/replan prompts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessEngine, task-profile compiler, executable graph/dependencies, TaskGraphActionMapper, tool/skill registries, persistent thread/workspace state, command/tool execution, safety constraints and model-call budgets. - Closure path: user request → thread/profile/graph context → live model selects operational graph additions/actions → runtime executes tools/workspace/subagent work → results/artifacts/world state enter noderesults/completion state → live replan can select additional actions → final synthesis/delivery reflects the changed operational state. - Boundary reachability: ThreadFirstSuperAgent.run() is a shipped standard entrypoint; it invokes compilegenerictaskpayload(...), passes the supported livemodel configuration, and executes the resulting graph through the credited thread runtime without requiring an adjacent development/evaluation system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the live model from the live-enabled mode removes the discretionary task-specific choice of model-generated graph additions/replan actions; deterministic fallback machinery remains, but it does not make materially the same open-ended decisions with the same discretion. - Evidence: app/harness/superagent.py; app/harness/taskprofile.py; app/agents/taskactions.py; app/agents/runtime.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: no-key mode has substantially more deterministic planning. A is credited to the supported live-enabled first-party operating mode, not to every execution configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination function is established at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate disturbances inspected included concurrent subagent/shared-workspace interference, skill-output conflict/redundancy, graph scheduling dependencies and parallel mission-track execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a specific disturbance among distinct S1 operational units. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task-graph dependency ordering, sequential skill execution, complementarity/conflict scoring, conflict/consensus reports, persistent shared thread state, and the optional parallel execution helper. - Closure path: no qualifying disturbance → coordination response → subsequent S1 behavior loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S2 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/agents/subagents.py; app/main.py; app/routing/complementarity.py; app/routing/executor.py; app/graph.py. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the currently executing job as a whole when present commitments no longer close the task, by deciding which additional operational commitments should be inserted before delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing required artifacts/channels/validation, command or tool failure, evidence/workspace gaps, incomplete current execution, and mismatches between the task specification and observed world state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose new current commitments from workspaceaction, toolcall, and subagent actions after seeing whole-job execution/completion state, and insert those commitments into the active graph. - Decision owner: the configured live model actor in livereplansuggestions() for the live-enabled mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic task/world-state construction, failure-policy classification, capability registry suggestions, fallback repair seed, node allow-lists, graph mutation, dependency rewiring, persistent execution state, scheduler/recovery machinery and tool/workspace enforcement. - Closure path: current graph executes → completion packet/world state exposes unresolved gap → graphreplan receives current job state → live model selects additional commitments → runtime appends the selected nodes and rewires synthesis/completion dependencies → subsequent execution performs the added work before the job can close. - Boundary reachability: the standard task-profile compiler adds a first-party graphreplan node whenever graph expansion enables replanning, and the standard thread-first execution path reaches TaskGraphActionMapper.executegraphreplan; supported livemodel configuration is carried through the same runtime context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the live model, the runtime can still apply deterministic fallback/capability repairs, but it no longer makes the same task-specific discretionary choice among allowed new commitments. The model therefore owns the open-ended current-control decision in the credited mode; the runtime enforces and persists it. - Evidence: app/harness/taskprofile.py; app/agents/taskactions.py; app/agents/runtime.py; app/harness/superagent.py; tests/testagentruntime.py as corroboration. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: S3 is mode-dependent; the autonomous owner exists in live-model-enabled execution. Deterministic recovery/scheduling alone would not justify A. - Whole-system current view: replan input includes query/graph id, failure policy, completion packet, stategap, capability replan, accumulated noderesults, execution-loop schema, current loop phase, phase summary and fallback seed; this is a job-wide current-state view rather than one worker&#39;s local result. - Current-control decision scope: add or withhold new tool calls, workspace actions and bounded subagent commitments, and thereby alter the active graph dependencies that gate synthesis/completion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary operational-audit path is established in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate audit surfaces inspected included the live critique pass, structured dissent/conflict verification, completion/validation nodes, traces, red-team/evaluator utilities, tests and report artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no complementary audit judgment with materially different access to operational reality is closed at the assessed runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critique prompts, evidence digest, deterministic conflict/dissent checks, validation artifacts, traces and development/evaluation utilities. - Closure path: not established for an independent audit channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S3 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/harness/liveagent.py; app/graph.py; app/routing/executor.py; adjacent tests/ and evaluator/red-team surfaces. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no organizational outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the assessed deployment boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate surfaces inspected included external evidence/search, runtime learning statistics, optimizer runs, live experiments, research-lab comparisons, strategy selection and skill reliability feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was found that turns external/future-relevant distinctions into organizational adaptation options and returns those options into present harness capability. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evidence providers, recordrun learning statistics, reliability-aware skill selection, optimizer/experiment result stores and research-lab tooling. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S4 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/memory/learning.py; app/routing/complementarity.py; app/harness/optimizer.py; app/harness/iteration.py; evidence/search paths in app/harness/evidence.py. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate surfaces inspected included system modes, risk/governance policy bundles, human approval/interrupt controls, constraints, recipes and static runtime settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was found for resolving an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue and returning that authoritative decision to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static PolicyBundle values, runtime constraints, safety checks, approval flags, operator interrupt/resume and configuration. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S5 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/policy/center.py; runtime constraints/settings in app/harness/models.py and app/harness/runtimesettings.py; thread control in app/agents/runtime.py. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f2b0fec6e23af8b058420f9cbad438f21ebcc4ab/assessments/haorui-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f2b0fec6e23af8b058420f9cbad438f21ebcc4ab\">f2b0fec &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T18:54:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/open-multi-agent/open-multi-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OMA / Open Multi-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/open-multi-agent/open-multi-agent/tree/4107fb144653c3240c569f345cf06b028dd7747a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4107fb144653c3240c569f345cf06b028dd7747a\">4107fb1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute assigned goals/tasks against model/tool environments and produce operational outcomes for the caller/run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous prompts and task requirements, model responses, tool/API results, dependency payloads, context state, structured-output failures and external-backend results requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next model-mediated action within a task — respond, invoke an allowed tool, consume observations, continue reasoning/delegation where enabled, and settle the task result. - Decision owner: the active OMA built-in LLM worker agent for standard built-in-agent modes; external process/ACP workers own their private inner loops and are not imported as first-party OMA decision actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent runner, model adapters, tool registry/grants, AgentPool, task queue, dependency/context injection, shared memory, budgets/timeouts, checkpoints and run-store fencing. - Closure path: task/prompt + current context → agent model selects action/tool → runtime executes/returns observation → observation re-enters agent context → agent selects further action or final result → task/run settlement and dependent release. - Boundary reachability: runAgent, runTasks and runTeam with built-in agents are first-party documented @open-multi-agent/core operating modes, not examples or CI-only paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven worker while leaving the scheduler, queue, stores, approval machinery and tool executor leaves no actor that makes the contextual task-level operational choices. Runtime machinery constrains and persists those decisions but does not substitute for them. - Evidence: packages/core/README.md, README.md, task-scheduling.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination loop was established for a concrete interference/oscillation among distinct OMA S1 worker units at the declared run/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate disturbances inspected included parallel ready tasks, competition for AgentPool capacity, duplicate processes advancing one logical run, dependency ordering and shared-memory writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific autonomous coordination discretion is supplied. Scheduler assignment/load balancing is whole-run resource/commitment control; run-store fencing prevents duplicate ownership of the same run; dependency edges sequence work; namespaced shared memory avoids key collisions. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPool semaphore, task dependencies, event-driven ready set, least-busy/composite scheduling, namespaced shared memory, run-store lease/fencing and deterministic task-status propagation. - Closure path: no first-party S2-specific feedback loop was found that observes an interaction disturbance among independent S1 workers and changes their subsequent local behaviour to damp it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple agents and parallel branches exist, but task assignment, dependency sequencing and capacity limits are not S2 merely because they coordinate execution. The reviewed mechanisms map more directly to S3 resource/current-control or duplicate-execution safety and do not expose an S2-specific autonomous decision path. - Evidence: task-scheduling.md, scheduler.ts, run-store.md, shared-memory.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments and interventions after operational outcomes reveal that the pending task graph should change. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failure, consensus rejection, successful outcomes that invalidate/reshape downstream work, assignee infeasibility and remaining budget/commitment constraints while other pending/blocked work still exists. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to keep the current pending graph or propose a PlanPatch that adds replacement tasks, retargets pending tasks or supersedes pending branches in response to a current task outcome. - Decision owner: OMA does not supply the autonomous decisive replanner. The developer/application supplies a Replanner or onTaskOutcome actor; that actor may itself call an LLM/service, but such autonomous composition remains application-owned. OMA owns validation, atomic application and execution closure after a proposal exists. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskOutcome snapshot of complete current task state and remaining budgets, deterministic Scheduler and AgentSelector, recovery limits, DAG validation, optional onPlanPatch gate, checkpoint persistence and event-driven publication of newly-ready work. - Closure path: task settles/reaches verification outcome → OMA constructs whole-run TaskOutcome → composed replanner proposes patch → OMA validates/gates and atomically applies accepted current-control changes → new/retargeted pending work enters the same scheduler and changes subsequent execution. - Whole-system current view: TaskOutcome.tasks exposes the full current task list together with current plan revision, triggering task/result/verification and remaining budget dimensions; scheduler decisions also inspect current DAG/load state. - Current-control decision scope: pending commitments may be appended, retargeted to another eligible agent, or superseded, thereby changing who will perform current work and which remaining commitments still count toward the run. - Boundary reachability: repairable recovery, Replanner, onTaskOutcome, PlanPatch validation and atomic application are documented first-party @open-multi-agent/core runtime surfaces reachable in runTasks/team execution; the missing element is the developer-composed decisive actor, which is why the state is C, not A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic scheduling already enforces current assignment/criticality/load policy, but no shipped autonomous actor owns the discretionary replan judgment. The first-party recovery seam is S3-specific and operationally closes a composed decision back into current execution, satisfying the narrow constructor threshold without pretending the scheduler itself is an autonomous S3 owner. - Evidence: adaptive-recovery.md, recovery.ts, task-scheduling.md, scheduler.ts. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the ordinary one-shot coordinator is not this S3 owner; it is explicitly absent mid-run. Durable plan/dispatch approval can constrain current work but OMA deliberately leaves reviewer product/transport/authorization to the integrator, so no separate parent-governed S3 mode is published here.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a proposed operational result and feed an audit judgment back into the result/control path before downstream use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but incorrect/incomplete task answers whose ordinary worker completion path would otherwise become accepted downstream state without an independent challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent judge agents decide accept/dissent against the original question and proposed answer; quorum and configured dissent policy determine whether the result is accepted, rejected or returned to the proposer for revision. - Decision owner: the configured OMA judge-agent roster owns the audit judgments; deterministic quorum parsing/aggregation and runtime gating transport those judgments. On onDissent: &#39;revise&#39;, the proposer remains the operational actor producing the corrected candidate, while judges retain the complementary audit right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runConsensus, per-task verify, separate judge invocations, structured {accept, critique} verdicts, quorum/max-round controls, dissent persistence, token/cost accounting, shared-memory verdict records and task-output replacement after an accepted revision. - Closure path: worker/proposer produces candidate → separate judges receive original question + candidate and issue verdicts/critiques → quorum/dissent policy resolves audit → dissent can be fed back to proposer for another round → an accepted revised answer supersedes task output and is propagated into queue/shared memory/downstream results. - Claim being audited: that the proposer/worker&#39;s candidate adequately answers the original task/question and should be accepted as the task result. - Ordinary reporting path: the worker/proposer&#39;s own final answer/result through the ordinary task completion path. - Complementary access path: distinct judge-agent invocations independently evaluate the candidate against the original question under refute or differentiated-lens prompts; they are not the worker&#39;s ordinary completion check and can use separately configured models/agents. - Independence boundary: judge agents are a separately configured verifier roster, invoked after/around the proposer result with their own model calls and structured verdict contract; they receive the candidate rather than inheriting the proposer&#39;s hidden reasoning state. Different judges can use different models and lens framings. The classification does not rely on generic traces, governance receipts or the post-run evaluation subsystem. - Who acts on findings: the first-party consensus loop aggregates judge decisions; under the default revise policy it returns dissent to the proposer, and per-task verification replaces downstream task state only when a revised candidate is accepted. - Boundary reachability: runConsensus() is a documented top-level core runtime mode and verify is a documented per-task hook in runTasks/runTeam; both are shipped first-party runtime paths rather than CI/eval-only machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the judge agents while leaving quorum code, traces and result plumbing removes the substantive independent audit judgments. Deterministic parsing and quorum enforcement cannot recreate critiques or accept/dissent decisions, so the decisive S3 feedback is agent-owned. - Evidence: consensus.md, coordinator.md, packages/core/README.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: governance-floor receipts and journal verification are useful audit mechanisms but are not the reason for A; post-run evaluation explicitly does not alter the business result. The positive mapping is the independent judge loop with corrective return.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime path was established that senses external/future-relevant change, develops adaptation options and returns a strategic capability change into present OMA operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate S4 inputs inspected included provider/tool failures, task outcomes, evaluation regressions/trends, routing profiles, model/provider choices and changing external requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime S4 adaptation judgment is supplied. Adaptive recovery changes the current unexecuted graph in response to current task outcomes (S3-constructor scope), while the evaluation subsystem measures completed runs without changing their business result. - Decision owner: none established for S4 in the assessed runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: offline/online evaluation reports and gates, model/execution routing, adaptive recovery, configuration, callbacks, provider fallback and application-owned policy code. - Closure path: no supported first-party outside/prospective sensing → option-generation → adaptation-decision → changed present capability loop was found inside the product-runtime boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluation can expose versions, regressions and trends, but its documented contract is observe-only relative to business runs; using its reports to change prompts/models/topology in CI or operator practice is an adjacent application/development loop unless separately wired by the integrator. - Evidence: evaluation.md, adaptive-recovery.md, packages/core/README.md. - Basis: explicit -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy escalation-decision-return loop was established at the OMA run/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate policy matters included declared governance roles, consequential-tool rules, egress policy, approval decisions, role/tool boundaries, budget ceilings and explicit topology overrides. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or operationally closed first-party parent authority resolves identity/ultimate-policy disputes for the system. Applications declare governance intent, tool grants, egress/budget policy and reviewer integration before/around execution. - Decision owner: application/developer/organization outside the assessed runtime owns ultimate policy; OMA deterministically validates/enforces selected rules and records deviations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: governanceIntent/required roles/order, execution receipts and governanceConclusion, consequential-tool classification/confirmation, egress policy, tool allow/deny lists, budgets, durable approval ledger, run-store cancellation and configuration. - Closure path: configured constraints and approval decisions can change/stop subsequent execution, but no first-party standard path carries an identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that authority&#39;s decision as S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: governance receipts prove that declared roles/order executed; they do not decide what the organization&#39;s identity or ultimate policy should be. Durable approvals bind exact reviewed content but OMA deliberately provides no reviewer product, routing, RBAC or escalation layer. These are strong enforcement/governance primitives without an S5 decision owner. - Evidence: tool-configuration.md, durable-approvals.md, observability.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: an organization can build a legitimate parent-policy layer on top of the durable approval/governance primitives; Methodology 0.3.5 does not publish S5 merely because a human can approve an operational action or because policy text is enforceable.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b4ce8b5a5cb50fb5ea1941fc7e0a2e73781ec6ea/assessments/open-multi-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b4ce8b5a5cb50fb5ea1941fc7e0a2e73781ec6ea\">b4ce8b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
